D('merry',{etymology:E('Middle English mirie, from Old English myrge “pleasant, agreeable”; the cheerful sense developed by about 1200.','merry'),contexts:[C('merry.s1','通用','The children stayed merry as they sang beside the lanterns.','孩子们在灯笼旁唱歌时一直兴高采烈。','stay + adjective describes a continuing state; merry describes their cheerful mood.','Merry is an adjective used after stay.','This is the cheerful sense, not the informal “slightly drunk” sense.')],mistakes:[M('The children merried beside the lanterns.','The children were merry beside the lanterns.','Merry is an adjective; it is not a verb that forms a past tense with -ed.','word form')],quiz:[Q('merry',1,1,'After the lively chorus, how did the children feel?','They felt cheerful and full of fun.','They felt slightly drunk.','They felt physically ill.','They felt cheerful and full of fun.','The scene describes a happy mood; the informal “slightly drunk” sense needs a drinking context.'),Q('merry',2,2,'At a dinner party, “a bit merry” most often suggests that someone is …','a little drunk.','very angry.','ready to leave.','a little drunk.','In informal use, merry can mean slightly affected by alcohol.'),Q('merry',3,1,'Which sentence uses merry to describe a cheerful occasion?','They had a merry evening around the fire.','They were gloomy after the disappointing announcement.','The room was gloomy throughout the evening.','They had a merry evening around the fire.','Merry naturally describes an enjoyable, cheerful occasion.'),Q('merry',4,2,'Which context best supports the informal sense of merry?','After several glasses of wine, he was pleasantly merry.','After the exam, she was merry because she passed.','The children were merry during the game.','After several glasses of wine, he was pleasantly merry.','The reference to alcohol points to “slightly drunk,” rather than simply cheerful.') ]});
D('mesh',{etymology:E('Late Middle English mesche “an opening in a net,” probably from Old English max; the verb developed from the noun.','mesh'),contexts:[C('mesh.s1','工业','The fine mesh keeps insects out while letting air circulate.','细密的网眼能挡住昆虫，同时让空气流通。','mesh is a mass/count noun for netting; keep + object + out describes prevention.','Fine modifies the noun mesh; the clause contrasts blocking insects with allowing air through.','The context uses the woven-net sense; mesh can also be a verb meaning fit together.')],mistakes:[M('The fine mash keeps insects out of the tent.','The fine mesh keeps insects out of the tent.','Mash means crushed food or material; mesh is the netting with open spaces.','confusable')],quiz:[Q('mesh',1,1,'What does fine mesh on a window do?','It forms a net with small openings.','It coats the glass with paint.','It makes the window opaque.','It forms a net with small openings.','Mesh is netting made from woven or joined strands.'),Q('mesh',2,1,'In “the mesh of the screen,” which word is closest in meaning?','netting','hinge','frame','netting','The openings and threads of a screen form a mesh.'),Q('mesh',3,1,'Which sentence uses mesh as a verb about gears?','The two gears mesh smoothly.','The two gears polish smoothly.','The two gears drip smoothly.','The two gears mesh smoothly.','As a verb, mesh can describe gear teeth engaging with one another.'),Q('mesh',4,1,'A plan and a budget “mesh” when they …','fit together well.','are made of wire.','remain completely unrelated.','fit together well.','The figurative verb means to fit or work together.') ]});
D('mesmerize',{etymology:E('Mesmerize was formed from mesmerism (1798), named for Austrian physician Franz Anton Mesmer; the sense “enthrall” is recorded by 1862.','mesmerize'),contexts:[C('mesmerize.s1','通用','The glassblower mesmerized the visitors as a glowing vase took shape.','玻璃工匠把发光的花瓶逐渐吹制成形，游客们看得入了迷。','mesmerize + object; the -ed verb describes the visitors’ captivated response.','Mesmerize is transitive here and takes visitors as its object.','The word means fascinate intensely, not literally put someone under medical hypnosis.')],mistakes:[M('The glassblower mesmerize the visitors for an hour.','The glassblower mesmerized the visitors for an hour.','A singular subject in the past-tense sentence requires mesmerized, not the bare form mesmerize.','verb-form')],quiz:[Q('mesmerize',1,1,'Why were the visitors mesmerized by the glassblower?','They watched the work with absorbed fascination.','They were forced to leave the studio.','They could not hear the glassblower.','They watched the work with absorbed fascination.','The word describes intense fascination that holds someone’s attention.'),Q('mesmerize',2,1,'Which scene best fits “the dancer mesmerized the audience”?','The audience watched, captivated by every movement.','The audience left before the music began.','The audience watched quietly without becoming absorbed.','The audience watched, captivated by every movement.','Mesmerize means to fascinate so strongly that people keep watching.'),Q('mesmerize',3,1,'In this context, mesmerize is closest to …','enthrall.','confuse.','interrupt.','enthrall.','The transferred modern sense is to captivate or enthrall.'),Q('mesmerize',4,1,'Which object can naturally follow mesmerize in a sentence?','The crowd','At midnight','With a blue scarf','The crowd','Mesmerize is transitive in this sense: a performer can mesmerize a crowd.') ]});
D('Mesolithic',{etymology:E('Nineteenth-century archaeological term built from Greek meso- “middle” and lithos “stone,” for the period between the Paleolithic and Neolithic.','Mesolithic'),contexts:[C('Mesolithic.s1','考古','The museum dates the flint tools to the Mesolithic period.','博物馆将这些燧石工具断代为中石器时代。','the Mesolithic period names an archaeological era; the adjective-like proper period name is capitalized.','Mesolithic modifies period and takes an initial capital as a named prehistoric period.','The sentence dates artifacts to the era, rather than using Mesolithic for the artifacts themselves.')],mistakes:[M('The museum dated the tools to the mesolithic period.','The museum dated the tools to the Mesolithic period.','The name of this archaeological period is conventionally capitalized.','capitalization')],quiz:[Q('Mesolithic',1,1,'Which period does Mesolithic name?','The middle phase of the Stone Age.','The age of the first written records.','The period of modern industrial machines.','The middle phase of the Stone Age.','Meso- means middle and lithic refers to stone; it falls between Old and New Stone Age periods.'),Q('Mesolithic',2,2,'What are “Mesolithic finds” in an excavation report?','Objects or remains from that prehistoric period.','Objects made during the Roman Empire.','Modern tools used by archaeologists.','Objects or remains from that prehistoric period.','Mesolithic can label tools, sites, and remains associated with the period.'),Q('Mesolithic',3,1,'Which sequence places the Mesolithic correctly?','Paleolithic, Mesolithic, Neolithic','Neolithic, Mesolithic, Paleolithic','Bronze Age, Mesolithic, Iron Age','Paleolithic, Mesolithic, Neolithic','The Mesolithic lies between the Old Stone Age and the New Stone Age.'),Q('Mesolithic',4,1,'Why is the word capitalized in “the Mesolithic”?','It names a recognized prehistoric period.','It is the first word of every sentence.','It is the surname of an archaeologist.','It names a recognized prehistoric period.','English convention capitalizes the name of this archaeological era.') ]});
D('meson',{etymology:E('Coined from Greek mesos “middle,” reflecting a particle with a mass intermediate between an electron and a proton.','meson'),contexts:[C('meson.s1','物理','In the experiment, each meson decayed before reaching the outer detector.','在实验中，每个介子到达外层探测器前就衰变了。','each + singular count noun; the verb decay describes an unstable particle.','Meson is a countable noun; its plural is mesons.','The sentence uses the particle-physics sense, not a generic small object.')],mistakes:[M('The detector recorded three mesonses.','The detector recorded three mesons.','Meson forms its plural with -s only; it does not take an extra -es.','plural')],quiz:[Q('meson',1,1,'In particle physics, what is a meson?','A short-lived particle in the hadron family.','A molecule made of two water atoms.','A permanently stable metal atom.','A short-lived particle in the hadron family.','A meson is a hadron composed of a quark and an antiquark and is often short-lived.'),Q('meson',2,1,'Which statement about mesons matches the source sense?','They are subatomic particles studied in particle physics.','They are tools for measuring temperature.','They are cells that carry oxygen.','They are subatomic particles studied in particle physics.','The entry defines meson as a particle, not an instrument, cell, or chemical molecule.'),Q('meson',3,1,'Choose the correct plural: “Pions are the lightest …”','mesons','mesones','meson','mesons','The regular plural of meson is mesons.'),Q('meson',4,1,'Which lab observation is consistent with an unstable meson?','It decays into other particles after a short time.','It grows into a larger atom.','It turns into a living cell.','It decays into other particles after a short time.','Mesons are short-lived subatomic particles that decay.') ]});
D('Mesozoic',{etymology:E('Nineteenth-century geological name from Greek mesos “middle” and zoion “animal,” referring to the middle era of animal life.','Mesozoic'),contexts:[C('Mesozoic.s1','地质','The limestone layers preserve marine fossils from the Mesozoic.','这些石灰岩层保存着中生代的海洋化石。','from the Mesozoic names the geological era to which the fossils belong.','Mesozoic is capitalized as the name of a geological era.','The word identifies the era; it does not mean any one fossil or dinosaur.')],mistakes:[M('The fossils were formed during the mesozoic.','The fossils were formed during the Mesozoic.','The name of the geological era is conventionally capitalized.','capitalization')],quiz:[Q('Mesozoic',1,1,'Which broad interval is the Mesozoic?','The era between the Paleozoic and Cenozoic.','The earliest part of human recorded history.','The current geological era.','The era between the Paleozoic and Cenozoic.','The Mesozoic is the middle of these three major geological eras.'),Q('Mesozoic',2,1,'Why is the Mesozoic often called the age of reptiles?','Reptiles, including dinosaurs, were especially prominent then.','The first mammals appeared only in modern times.','It was a period before any land animals existed.','Reptiles, including dinosaurs, were especially prominent then.','The era is known for the dominance and diversity of reptiles, especially dinosaurs.'),Q('Mesozoic',3,1,'A fossil labeled Mesozoic most likely dates to …','the interval after the Paleozoic and before the Cenozoic.','the last few centuries.','the period after the Cenozoic.','the interval after the Paleozoic and before the Cenozoic.','Those are the Mesozoic era’s position and boundaries in the geological sequence.'),Q('Mesozoic',4,1,'Which sentence uses Mesozoic as an era name?','Dinosaurs flourished during the Mesozoic.','The Mesozoic measured the rock sample.','She bought a Mesozoic for the field trip.','Dinosaurs flourished during the Mesozoic.','The word is a proper geological era name, often used after during or in.') ]});
D('mesquite',{etymology:E('Borrowed through Spanish mezquite from Nahuatl mizquitl, the name of the drought-tolerant leguminous tree.','mesquite'),contexts:[C('mesquite.s1','生物','The ranchers planted mesquite along the dry pasture because it tolerates drought.','牧场主在干旱牧场边种了牧豆树，因为它耐旱。','mesquite is a count noun for a drought-tolerant leguminous tree; it tolerates drought explains the choice.','The singular plant name follows planted; mesquite wood is another common use.','The example concerns the plant itself, not just its flavor as barbecue smoke.')],mistakes:[M('Mesquite trees need constantly flooded soil to survive.','Mesquite trees tolerate dry conditions and can survive drought.','The source sense identifies mesquite as drought-tolerant; constant flooding reverses that defining trait.','meaning')],quiz:[Q('mesquite',1,1,'What kind of plant is a mesquite?','A drought-tolerant legume.','A water lily that grows only in ponds.','A tropical orchid with no woody stem.','A drought-tolerant legume.','Mesquite is a leguminous shrub or small tree adapted to dry conditions.'),Q('mesquite',2,1,'Why might mesquite be planted on an arid ranch?','It can tolerate dry conditions.','It requires daily flooding.','It cannot grow in warm climates.','It can tolerate dry conditions.','Drought tolerance makes mesquite suitable for dry areas.'),Q('mesquite',3,1,'What can mesquite wood be used for?','Fuel and smoke for barbecuing.','Making paper from seaweed only.','Cooling water in a pipe.','Fuel and smoke for barbecuing.','The wood is commonly used as fuel and for its smoke flavor.'),Q('mesquite',4,1,'Which sentence uses mesquite as the plant name?','A mesquite shaded the path near the ranch.','The cook added mesquite smoke to the meat.','The cook measured the meat with a mesquite.','A mesquite shaded the path near the ranch.','Here mesquite names the drought-tolerant plant; the second option refers to its wood or smoke.') ]});
D('mess',{etymology:E('Middle English mes, from Old French mes “portion of food,” from Latin missum “a course, dish,” later extended to untidy conditions and difficult situations.','mess'),contexts:[C('mess.s2','口语','The rushed handover left the night team with a difficult mess to sort out.','仓促的交接给夜班团队留下了一团难以收拾的麻烦。','leave + object + with a mess; here the noun refers to a difficult situation.','Mess is a count noun in a difficult mess; sort out means resolve it.','This is the difficult-situation sense, distinct from literal dirt or disorder.')],mistakes:[M('There are a mess to clean in the kitchen.','There is a mess to clean in the kitchen.','In this sentence the intended meaning is “caused a problem”; mess cannot take this construction without a particle such as mess up.','grammar')],quiz:[Q('mess',1,1,'A kitchen covered with spilled flour and dirty dishes is …','a mess.','a message.','a method.','a mess.','Mess as a noun can mean a dirty or untidy condition.'),Q('mess',2,2,'“The budget is a mess” most likely means that …','the budget situation is difficult or badly managed.','the budget is a clean dining room.','the budget is a person carrying letters.','the budget situation is difficult or badly managed.','Mess also means a difficult situation caused by mistakes.'),Q('mess',3,3,'In “Don’t mess up the schedule,” what does mess up mean?','make it untidy or cause it to go wrong.','send it as a short note.','measure it carefully.','make it untidy or cause it to go wrong.','As a verb, mess up means to spoil, disrupt, or make something unsuccessful.'),Q('mess',4,1,'Which use of mess is a noun describing disorder?','The garage was a complete mess.','Please mess the onions into a bowl.','The garage messes the mechanic yesterday.','The garage was a complete mess.','A complete mess is a noun phrase describing an untidy state.') ]});
D('message',{etymology:E('Middle English message, from Old French message and Late Latin missaticum, from Latin mittere “to send.”','message'),contexts:[C('message.s2','文学','The novel’s message is that small acts of kindness can change a community.','这部小说传达的主旨是，小小的善举也能改变一个社区。','the message of a work names its central idea; that-clause states the message.','Message is a count noun here; possessive novel’s identifies the work.','This is the central-idea sense, not a sent text or the verb “to message.”')],mistakes:[M('The novel’s message are that small acts matter.','The novel’s message is that small acts matter.','The singular head noun message takes the singular verb is.','grammar')],quiz:[Q('message',1,1,'“I left a message on her phone” refers to …','a short piece of information sent or left for her.','the phone’s battery level.','the main character in a novel.','a short piece of information sent or left for her.','A message can be a short communication left for someone.'),Q('message',2,2,'What is the message of a speech?','Its main idea or point.','The paper on which it is printed.','The person who delivers it.','Its main idea or point.','Message can mean the central idea a speech or book communicates.'),Q('message',3,3,'In “Message me when you arrive,” message means …','send me a message.','deliver a parcel to my home.','remember my name.','send me a message.','Message is also a verb meaning to send someone a message.'),Q('message',4,2,'Which sentence uses message to mean a central theme?','The film’s message is that hope can survive hardship.','I sent her a message with the meeting time.','He messaged the driver at noon.','The film’s message is that hope can survive hardship.','A film’s message is its main communicative idea or theme.') ]});
D('messenger',{etymology:E('Middle English messager, from Old French messagier, formed from message; the word names a person who carries or delivers a message.','messenger'),contexts:[C('messenger.s1','职业','A messenger carried the signed contract across town before the offices closed.','一名信使在办公室关门前把签好的合同送到了城另一头。','a messenger + past-tense action; carried identifies the person’s delivery role.','Messenger is a count noun for a person who carries or delivers messages.','The example uses the human carrier sense, not a messaging app.')],mistakes:[M('A messenger is the short note left on a phone.','A message is the short note left on a phone.','A messenger carries or delivers a message; “messaged by foot” does not express that action.','word choice')],quiz:[Q('messenger',1,1,'Who is a messenger in the traditional sense?','A person who carries a message to someone.','A person who writes a dictionary.','A person who receives the message.','A person who carries a message to someone.','A messenger is a person who carries or delivers a communication.'),Q('messenger',2,1,'A messenger arrives with a sealed letter. What is the person doing?','Delivering information or correspondence.','Receiving the sealed letter from the sender.','Writing the original letter rather than carrying it.','Delivering information or correspondence.','The role centers on carrying and delivering a message.'),Q('messenger',3,1,'Which phrase is a natural use of messenger?','a royal messenger','a messenger of the ruler’s lettered','a messengerly office','a royal messenger','Messenger is a noun for the person sent to carry communications.'),Q('messenger',4,1,'Which word names the message carrier rather than the communication?','messenger','message','messaging','messenger','The suffix -er here names a person who carries a message; message names the communication.') ]});
D('messy',{etymology:E('Formed from mess + -y, the adjective suffix meaning “characterized by” or “full of.”','messy'),contexts:[C('messy.s2','通用','Negotiating the boundary with three owners became a messy dispute.','与三位业主协商边界变成了一场棘手的争端。','messy modifies dispute and means difficult or unpleasant to deal with.','Messy is an adjective before the noun dispute.','The context tests the figurative difficult-situation sense, not physical dirt.')],mistakes:[M('The negotiation was a messsy dispute.','The negotiation was a messy dispute.','Messy has one s after the e and a doubled s before -y; messsy adds an extra s.','spelling')],quiz:[Q('messy',1,1,'A messy room is …','dirty and untidy.','quiet and empty.','large and expensive.','dirty and untidy.','The literal adjective sense describes something dirty or untidy.'),Q('messy',2,2,'A messy divorce is likely to be …','difficult and unpleasant to deal with.','very clean and orderly.','short and entirely private.','difficult and unpleasant to deal with.','Messy has a figurative sense for complicated, unpleasant situations.'),Q('messy',3,1,'Which noun can messy naturally describe in its literal sense?','a kitchen floor covered in paint','a perfectly organized shelf','a silent telephone','a kitchen floor covered in paint','Messy can describe a physically dirty or untidy place.'),Q('messy',4,2,'Which sentence uses the figurative sense of messy?','The lawsuit became a messy fight over ownership.','The messy socks were on the floor.','She washed the messy plate.','The lawsuit became a messy fight over ownership.','A legal fight can be messy because it is difficult and unpleasant to resolve.') ]});
D('metabolic',{etymology:E('From metabolism + -ic, ultimately from Greek metabolikos “changeable” and metabole “change.”','metabolic'),contexts:[C('metabolic.s1','医学','The doctor measured her metabolic rate before changing the treatment plan.','医生在调整治疗方案前测量了她的代谢率。','metabolic modifies rate in the compound metabolic rate.','Metabolic is an adjective; the related noun is metabolism.','This is the biological sense relating to the body’s conversion of substances and energy.')],mistakes:[M('The patient’s metabolic was measured every morning.','The patient’s metabolism was measured every morning.','Metabolic is an adjective; the noun naming the body’s chemical processes is metabolism.','word form')],quiz:[Q('metabolic',1,1,'A metabolic process is connected with …','the body’s chemical conversion of substances and energy.','the body’s chemical activity.','the repair of metal tools.','the body’s chemical conversion of substances and energy.','Metabolic describes processes involved in metabolism.'),Q('metabolic',2,1,'What does metabolic modify in “metabolic rate”?','rate','ratefully','metabolism rate as an adjective phrase','rate','Metabolic is the adjective that modifies rate in the standard phrase.'),Q('metabolic',3,1,'Which phrase uses metabolic in a medical context?','metabolic activity in liver cells','metabolic rainfall over the coast','metabolic gears in a clock','metabolic activity in liver cells','Liver cells carry out metabolism, so metabolic activity is a natural collocation.'),Q('metabolic',4,1,'Choose the noun form related to metabolic:','metabolism','metabolize','metabolically','metabolism','Metabolism is the noun; metabolic is its adjective form.') ]});
D('metabolism',{etymology:E('From Greek metabolē “change,” via modern scientific usage; the word names the body’s chemical processes of conversion.','metabolism'),contexts:[C('metabolism.s1','生物','During fasting, the body’s metabolism continues to use stored energy.','禁食期间，身体的新陈代谢仍会利用储存的能量。','the body’s metabolism is the subject; use stored energy describes what it does.','Metabolism is generally an uncountable noun for the linked bodily processes.','The example is biological metabolism, not a general method of change.')],mistakes:[M('Her metabolism are affected by the medicine.','Her metabolism is affected by the medicine.','Metabolism is a singular noun here and takes is, not are.','grammar')],quiz:[Q('metabolism',1,1,'What does metabolism refer to in biology?','The body’s processes for converting substances and energy.','The body’s mechanical movement rather than its chemical processes.','the rate at which food moves through the gut without chemical change.','The body’s processes for converting substances and energy.','Metabolism is the set of chemical processes that sustain the body.'),Q('metabolism',2,1,'Which statement about metabolism is accurate?','It includes processes that turn food into usable energy and tissue.','It is the name of a person who studies metals.','It stops completely whenever a person sleeps.','It includes processes that turn food into usable energy and tissue.','The source sense describes how the body converts food into energy and tissue.'),Q('metabolism',3,1,'Which collocation is natural?','a slow metabolism','a metabolism of the microscope','a metabolism letter','a slow metabolism','Metabolism commonly appears with adjectives such as slow or fast.'),Q('metabolism',4,1,'In “Exercise can speed up metabolism,” what may become faster?','The body’s chemical processing of substances.','The speed of a message delivery.','The growth of a metal mesh.','The body’s chemical processing of substances.','Speed up metabolism refers to the body’s chemical processes.') ]});
D('metabolize',{etymology:E('Formed from metabolism + -ize; the verb means to process a substance through metabolism.','metabolize'),contexts:[C('metabolize.s1','医学','The liver metabolizes caffeine, though the rate varies from person to person.','肝脏会代谢咖啡因，不过代谢速度因人而异。','metabolize + substance; the liver is the agent and caffeine the object.','Metabolize is a transitive verb; the third-person singular form is metabolizes.','The context describes biochemical processing, not simply swallowing or digesting.')],mistakes:[M('The liver metabolize caffeine at different rates.','The liver metabolizes caffeine at different rates.','The singular subject liver requires the third-person singular verb metabolizes.','verb-form')],quiz:[Q('metabolize',1,1,'If the body metabolizes a drug, it …','chemically processes or converts it.','absorbs it unchanged without chemical processing.','moves it through the body without chemical change.','chemically processes or converts it.','To metabolize is to break down or convert a substance through metabolism.'),Q('metabolize',2,1,'Which organ commonly metabolizes many medicines?','the liver','the eardrum','the kneecap','the liver','The liver plays a major role in processing many drugs.'),Q('metabolize',3,1,'Choose the correct verb: “Some people ___ alcohol slowly.”','metabolize','metabolizes','metabolizing','metabolize','The plural subject people takes the base form metabolize.'),Q('metabolize',4,1,'Which is closest in meaning to metabolize in this context?','process chemically in the body','send by courier','measure with a ruler','process chemically in the body','Metabolize refers to the chemical conversion of a substance in the body.') ]});
D('metal',{etymology:E('Middle English metal, from Old French metal and Latin metallum, from Greek metallon “mine, quarry, metal.”','metal'),contexts:[C('metal.s2','材料','The gate is made of metal.','工坊给门装上了一个金属把手。','metal modifies handle and means made of metal.','In this use metal is an attributive noun functioning as an adjective.','The sentence tests the “made of metal” modifier sense rather than metal as a material noun.')],mistakes:[M('The metal gate were badly bent.','The metal gate was badly bent.','The singular subject gate takes was rather than were.','grammar')],quiz:[Q('metal',1,1,'Which material is metal?','Copper','Cotton','Clay','Copper','Copper is a metal; cotton and clay are not metals.'),Q('metal',2,2,'In “a metal water bottle,” metal means …','made of metal.','containing metallic music.','measured in metres.','made of metal.','Metal is used attributively before bottle to show its material.'),Q('metal',3,1,'Which item is most clearly made of metal?','an iron gate','a wool scarf','a paper envelope','an iron gate','Iron is a metal, so an iron gate is made of metal.'),Q('metal',4,1,'Which sentence uses metal as a material noun?','Steel is a strong metal.','She bought a metal lunchbox.','They metaled the window.','Steel is a strong metal.','Here metal names a class of solid materials; in the second sentence it modifies lunchbox.') ]});
D('metallic',{etymology:E('From French métallique or Latin metallicus, based on metallum “metal”; the adjective describes metal or a metal-like quality.','metallic'),contexts:[C('metallic.s1','通用','A metallic taste lingered after the dentist polished the filling.','牙医抛光补牙材料后，嘴里还留着一股金属味。','metallic modifies taste and describes a quality resembling metal.','Metallic is an adjective; it can describe material as well as appearance, sound, or taste.','The example uses the metal-like quality sense rather than an object literally made of metal.')],mistakes:[M('The bell gave a metalically ring.','The bell gave a metallic ring.','Metallic is the adjective needed before ring; metalically is not the standard form.','word form')],quiz:[Q('metallic',1,1,'A metallic taste is a taste that …','resembles the taste associated with metal.','is sweet like honey.','is completely odorless and tasteless.','resembles the taste associated with metal.','Metallic can describe a taste or quality that seems like metal.'),Q('metallic',2,1,'Which phrase uses metallic to describe appearance?','a metallic sheen on the paint','a metal worker at the forge','a metre of blue cloth','a metallic sheen on the paint','Metallic can mean looking like metal, as in a reflective sheen.'),Q('metallic',3,1,'Which noun can metallic naturally modify?','sound','quickly','underneath','sound','Metallic commonly describes a ringing or harsh sound that resembles metal.'),Q('metallic',4,1,'How does metallic differ from metal in “a metallic shine”?','Metallic describes a metal-like quality; metal names the material.','Metallic is a verb meaning to polish.','Metallic means measured in metres.','Metallic describes a metal-like quality; metal names the material.','The adjective metallic can signal resemblance, while metal names the substance itself.') ]});
D('metalloid',{etymology:E('From Greek metallon “metal” + -oid from eidos “form, likeness,” for an element with partly metallic and partly nonmetallic properties.','metalloid'),contexts:[C('metalloid.s2','化学','Silicon is a metalloid used in many semiconductor devices.','硅是一种用于许多半导体器件的类金属元素。','a metalloid is a count noun naming an element; used in devices describes its application.','The noun metalloid takes the article a and plural -s.','This is the element noun sense; the adjective sense means having metalloid properties.')],mistakes:[M('Arsenic is a metalloid, so it is an ordinary metal with no nonmetallic properties.','Arsenic is a metalloid, so it has some metallic and some nonmetallic properties.','Calling an element a metalloid means it has a mix of properties, not that it is a typical metal.','meaning')],quiz:[Q('metalloid',1,1,'What does the adjective metalloid mean?','Having some metallic and some nonmetallic properties.','Made entirely of pure gold.','Containing no metal-like qualities at all.','Having some metallic and some nonmetallic properties.','The adjective describes a mixture of metal-like and nonmetal-like properties.'),Q('metalloid',2,2,'A metalloid such as silicon is …','an element that is neither a typical metal nor a typical nonmetal.','a type of woven steel net.','a compound made only of oxygen.','an element that is neither a typical metal nor a typical nonmetal.','As a noun, metalloid names elements such as silicon, arsenic, or boron.'),Q('metalloid',3,2,'Which sentence uses metalloid as a noun?','Boron is a metalloid.','The alloy looks metalloid under the light.','The wire metalloid into a ring.','Boron is a metalloid.','The phrase a metalloid identifies boron as an element in that category.'),Q('metalloid',4,1,'Why is silicon often classified as a metalloid?','It shares some traits with metals and some with nonmetals.','It is a common noble gas.','It is a metal that contains no atoms.','It shares some traits with metals and some with nonmetals.','Metalloids have an intermediate set of properties.') ]});
D('metallurgy',{etymology:E('From Greek metallourgos “working metal,” built on metallon “metal” and ergon “work”; metallurgy names the study and technology of metals.','metallurgy'),contexts:[C('metallurgy.s1','工程','The course compares ancient metallurgy with modern alloy production.','这门课程比较了古代冶金术与现代合金生产。','metallurgy is the subject being compared; with introduces the contrasting practice.','Metallurgy is normally an uncountable discipline noun.','The word covers extracting and working metals and understanding their structures and alloys.')],mistakes:[M('She enrolled in a metallurgy to learn how metals are extracted.','She enrolled in metallurgy to learn how metals are extracted.','The discipline name metallurgy is uncountable and does not normally take a here.','countability')],quiz:[Q('metallurgy',1,1,'What does metallurgy study?','The extraction, structure, working, and alloys of metals.','The history of weather forecasting.','The grammar of scientific writing.','The extraction, structure, working, and alloys of metals.','Metallurgy studies metals and how they are extracted, structured, and worked.'),Q('metallurgy',2,1,'Which person is most likely to study metallurgy?','An engineer researching how to make stronger alloys.','A meteorologist tracking storms.','A linguist comparing metaphors.','An engineer researching how to make stronger alloys.','Alloy design and metal processing are central metallurgy topics.'),Q('metallurgy',3,1,'Which phrase is a natural collocation?','metallurgy of bronze production','metallurgy of lunchtime messages','metallurgy the room clean','metallurgy of bronze production','Bronze production involves metalworking and alloys, a natural subject for metallurgy.'),Q('metallurgy',4,1,'Is metallurgy normally countable in “She studies ___”?','No; use “She studies metallurgy.”','Yes; always use “a metallurgy.”','Only in the plural “metallurgies.”','No; use “She studies metallurgy.”','As an academic field, metallurgy is generally an uncountable noun.') ]});
D('metalwork',{etymology:E('Compound of metal + work, used for metal objects made by craft and for the craft or activity of shaping metal.','metalwork'),contexts:[C('metalwork.s1','工艺','The conservator cleaned the intricate metalwork around the old doorway.','修复师清理了旧门口周围精巧的金属装饰构件。','metalwork names a collection of metal pieces; around the doorway locates the work.','Metalwork can be a collective mass noun for metal objects or craftsmanship.','This is the pieces-of-metal sense; metalwork can also mean the activity of making them.')],mistakes:[M('The metalwork around the gate are beautiful.','The metalwork around the gate is beautiful.','Metalwork is a collective noun for the crafted pieces here, so it takes the singular verb is.','grammar')],quiz:[Q('metalwork',1,1,'In “the restored metalwork on the gate,” metalwork means …','metal pieces or fittings made by craft.','a worker who carries messages.','the study of body chemistry.','metal pieces or fittings made by craft.','Metalwork can collectively name decorative or structural metal pieces.'),Q('metalwork',2,1,'A school course in metalwork teaches students to …','shape and join metal.','classify clouds.','write text messages.','shape and join metal.','Metalwork also names the craft or activity of making metal objects.'),Q('metalwork',3,1,'Which phrase naturally uses metalwork for crafted objects?','ornate metalwork on the balcony','a metalwork carried the parcel','to metalwork the afternoon','ornate metalwork on the balcony','Metalwork can be an uncountable collective noun for decorative metal pieces.'),Q('metalwork',4,1,'Which statement captures both common senses of metalwork?','It can mean crafted metal pieces or the work of making them.','It only means a person who repairs watches.','It means a small metallic-sounding particle.','It can mean crafted metal pieces or the work of making them.','The entry includes both metal objects and the craft of producing them.') ]});
D('metalworker',{etymology:E('Compound of metal + worker; worker uses work + -er to name a person who shapes or fabricates metal.','metalworker'),contexts:[C('metalworker.s1','工艺','The metalworker forged a replacement hinge for the restored gate.','金属工匠为修复后的大门锻造了一个替换铰链。','metalworker is the agent; forged describes the skilled metal-forming work.','Metalworker is a count noun for a craftsperson who forms or joins metal.','The example shows fabrication by a person, rather than the finished metalwork itself.')],mistakes:[M('The metalworker are fitting a bronze hinge.','The metalworker is fitting a bronze hinge.','The singular count noun metalworker takes is, not are.','grammar')],quiz:[Q('metalworker',1,1,'Who is a metalworker?','A craftsperson who cuts, joins, or forms metal.','A scientist who studies small particles only.','A person who carries letters on foot.','A craftsperson who cuts, joins, or forms metal.','A metalworker makes or repairs things from metal.'),Q('metalworker',2,1,'Which task best fits a metalworker?','Welding a steel frame.','Forecasting tomorrow’s rainfall.','Editing a novel’s theme.','Welding a steel frame.','Joining metal parts is work a metalworker may do.'),Q('metalworker',3,1,'Choose the correct sentence about one metalworker.','The metalworker repairs iron railings.','The metalworker repair iron railings.','The metalworker are repairing iron railings.','The metalworker repairs iron railings.','The singular subject takes the third-person singular verb repairs.'),Q('metalworker',4,1,'Which word names the person rather than the craft or its products?','metalworker','metalwork','metallurgy','metalworker','Metalworker names the person; metalwork names pieces or the craft, and metallurgy names the field.') ]});
D('metamorphic',{etymology:E('From metamorphosis + -ic, ultimately Greek meta- “change” + morphē “form”; in geology it describes rock changed by heat, pressure, or fluids.','metamorphic'),contexts:[C('metamorphic.s1','地质','Under intense heat and pressure, the original limestone became metamorphic marble.','原始石灰岩在强热和高压作用下变成了变质大理岩。','metamorphic modifies marble and identifies a rock altered from an earlier type.','Metamorphic is an adjective; metamorphic rock is the standard geology phrase.','The example concerns geological transformation, not a sudden change in a person’s character.')],mistakes:[M('Marble formed from limestone is a metamorphosis rock.','Marble formed from limestone is a metamorphic rock.','Metamorphic is the adjective for changed rock; metamorphosis is the noun for a complete change.','word form')],quiz:[Q('metamorphic',1,1,'What makes a rock metamorphic?','It recrystallized under heat, pressure, or active fluids.','It cooled directly from a star.','It consists only of loose beach sand.','It recrystallized under heat, pressure, or active fluids.','Metamorphic rocks are changed by heat, pressure, or chemically active fluids.'),Q('metamorphic',2,1,'Marble formed from limestone is an example of …','metamorphic rock.','a meteorite.','a microbe.','metamorphic rock.','Limestone can recrystallize into marble under metamorphic conditions.'),Q('metamorphic',3,1,'Which adjective belongs in “___ rock”?','metamorphic','metamorphosis','metamorphically','metamorphic','Metamorphic is the adjective that modifies rock.'),Q('metamorphic',4,1,'Which process can produce metamorphic rock?','Pressure changes the minerals in existing rock.','A plant absorbs sunlight.','A message is sent across town.','Pressure changes the minerals in existing rock.','Heat and pressure can transform existing rock into metamorphic rock.') ]});
D('metamorphosis',{etymology:E('From Greek metamorphōsis “a transforming,” from meta- “change” + morphē “form.”','metamorphosis'),contexts:[C('metamorphosis.s1','生物','The caterpillar’s metamorphosis into a butterfly takes place inside a chrysalis.','毛毛虫在蛹中完成向蝴蝶的蜕变。','metamorphosis into names the resulting form; the sentence describes a complete biological change.','Metamorphosis is a count noun; its plural is metamorphoses.','This is the biological transformation sense; it can also describe a radical change in character.')],mistakes:[M('The metamorphosis from tadpole to frog take weeks.','The metamorphosis from tadpole to frog takes weeks.','The singular subject metamorphosis takes the singular verb takes.','grammar')],quiz:[Q('metamorphosis',1,1,'A caterpillar changing into a butterfly undergoes …','metamorphosis.','meteorology.','metallurgy.','metamorphosis.','Metamorphosis is a complete change of form such as a caterpillar’s transformation.'),Q('metamorphosis',2,1,'Which sentence uses metamorphosis figuratively?','Years abroad brought a metamorphosis in her outlook.','The forecast showed a metamorphosis of rainfall in millimetres.','The metalworker called a hinge a metamorphosis.','Years abroad brought a metamorphosis in her outlook.','The noun also describes a radical change in character or condition.'),Q('metamorphosis',3,1,'Choose the correct plural form:','metamorphoses','metamorphosises','metamorphos','metamorphoses','The plural of metamorphosis changes -sis to -ses: metamorphoses.'),Q('metamorphosis',4,1,'Which word best completes “the ___ from tadpole to frog”?','metamorphosis','metamorphic','metamorphically','metamorphosis','The noun metamorphosis names the complete biological change.') ]});
D('metaphor',{etymology:E('From Greek metaphora “a transfer,” from metapherein “to transfer,” from meta- “across” + pherein “to carry.”','metaphor'),contexts:[C('metaphor.s1','语言','Calling the crowded city “a beehive” is a metaphor that stresses constant activity.','把拥挤的城市称作“蜂巢”是一种强调忙碌活动的隐喻。','a metaphor describes one thing as another; the that-clause explains the comparison’s effect.','Metaphor is a count noun; it does not use like or as to make the comparison.','This is a figure of speech; a metaphor can also be an object regarded as a symbol.')],mistakes:[M('“The city is like a beehive” is a metaphor without like.','“The city is a beehive” is a metaphor; “like a beehive” is a simile.','An explicit comparison with like is conventionally a simile; a metaphor identifies one thing as another.','distinction')],quiz:[Q('metaphor',1,1,'“Time is a thief” is a metaphor because it …','describes time as if it were a thief.','reports that a thief stole a clock.','compares time using the word like.','describes time as if it were a thief.','A metaphor describes one thing as another without a literal identity.'),Q('metaphor',2,2,'If a journey in a novel is called a metaphor for life, the journey is …','a symbol representing life.','a real trip taken by every reader.','a figure measuring distance.','a symbol representing life.','Metaphor can also mean something regarded as a symbol of another thing.'),Q('metaphor',3,1,'Which line is a metaphor rather than a simile?','Her voice was velvet.','Her voice was like velvet.','Her voice was louder than mine.','Her voice was velvet.','The first identifies voice with velvet; the second uses like and is a simile.'),Q('metaphor',4,1,'What does a metaphor do?','It represents one thing through another.','It gives a literal scientific measurement.','It names the person who delivers a message.','It represents one thing through another.','A metaphor transfers meaning by describing one thing as another.') ]});
D('metaphorical',{etymology:E('From metaphor + -ical, ultimately from Greek metaphora “transfer”; it describes an expression used figuratively rather than literally.','metaphorical'),contexts:[C('metaphorical.s1','语言学','The “wall” between the two teams was metaphorical: they could meet in person.','两队之间的“墙”只是比喻性的：他们可以当面见面。','metaphorical modifies wall and signals that the barrier is figurative, not physical.','Metaphorical is an adjective; it can precede a noun or follow be.','The clause confirms a nonliteral barrier because the teams can meet face to face.')],mistakes:[M('The teams faced a metaphorical wall of concrete bricks.','The teams faced a physical wall of concrete bricks.','A wall made of concrete bricks is literal, so physical is the accurate adjective.','meaning')],quiz:[Q('metaphorical',1,1,'A metaphorical barrier between teams is …','an imagined or figurative obstacle.','a concrete wall separating two rooms.','a unit for measuring length.','an imagined or figurative obstacle.','Metaphorical marks a comparison or expression that is not literal.'),Q('metaphorical',2,1,'Which phrase uses metaphorical correctly?','a metaphorical prison that represents social pressure','a figurative prison representing limited opportunities','a metaphorical measured in metres','a metaphorical prison that represents social pressure','The adjective suits a figurative prison used to represent an abstract constraint.'),Q('metaphorical',3,1,'Which adjective contrasts with metaphorical in “a ___ wall”?','literal','metaphor','metaphorically','literal','Literal means actual rather than figurative, contrasting with metaphorical.'),Q('metaphorical',4,1,'What does metaphorical tell a reader about “a storm of criticism”?','The storm is figurative criticism, not weather.','The criticism came with rain and thunder.','The phrase names a weather scientist.','The storm is figurative criticism, not weather.','Metaphorical language compares criticism to a storm without claiming actual weather.') ]});
D('metaphorically',{etymology:E('From metaphorical + -ly, the adverb form of metaphorical, ultimately based on Greek metaphora “transfer.”','metaphorically'),contexts:[C('metaphorically.s1','通用','Metaphorically speaking, the new bridge gave the divided neighborhood a way forward.','打个比方说，这座新桥为分裂的社区带来了前进的道路。','metaphorically speaking signals that way forward is a figurative expression.','Metaphorically is an adverb modifying speaking or the interpretation of the whole statement.','The bridge may be real, but the “way forward” means renewed cooperation.')],mistakes:[M('Metaphorical speaking, the bridge gave the neighborhood a way forward.','Metaphorically speaking, the bridge gave the neighborhood a way forward.','The adverb metaphorically modifies speaking; metaphorical is an adjective and cannot fill this role.','word form')],quiz:[Q('metaphorically',1,1,'Why might a writer begin “Metaphorically speaking, …”?','To show that the following expression is not literal.','To report a weather forecast.','To quote a legal document exactly.','To show that the following expression is not literal.','The adverb warns readers to interpret the statement figuratively.'),Q('metaphorically',2,1,'In “The project was a marathon, metaphorically,” the project …','took sustained effort but was not a literal race.','was an official 42-kilometre running event.','measured exactly 42 metres.','took sustained effort but was not a literal race.','Metaphorically marks marathon as a figurative description of sustained effort.'),Q('metaphorically',3,1,'Choose the correct adverb: “She described the policy ___ as a safety net.”','metaphorically','metaphorical','metaphor','metaphorically','The adverb metaphorically modifies described and signals figurative wording.'),Q('metaphorically',4,1,'Which sentence uses metaphorically to prevent a literal reading?','Metaphorically, the team climbed a mountain of paperwork.','The team climbed a mountain in the Alps.','The team measured the mountain with a metre.','Metaphorically, the team climbed a mountain of paperwork.','The paperwork mountain is an image for a very large workload.') ]});
D('metaphysical',{etymology:E('From medieval Latin metaphysica, from Greek ta meta ta physika “the things after the Physics,” the title of Aristotle’s work on first principles.','metaphysical'),contexts:[C('metaphysical.s1','哲学','The story turns a family mystery into a metaphysical question about whether identity survives memory loss.','这个故事把家族谜团引向一个关于失去记忆后身份是否仍能延续的形而上学问题。','metaphysical modifies question and signals a topic beyond ordinary physical science.','Metaphysical is an adjective; metaphysics is the related field noun.','The question concerns existence and identity, not a laboratory measurement.')],mistakes:[M('She raised a metaphysical questions about the soul.','She raised metaphysical questions about the soul.','The plural noun questions cannot take the singular article a in this construction.','grammar')],quiz:[Q('metaphysical',1,1,'A metaphysical question is mainly about …','existence, identity, or causation beyond physical science.','the exact temperature of a metal sample.','the spelling of a street name.','existence, identity, or causation beyond physical science.','Metaphysical describes questions about fundamental existence and reality.'),Q('metaphysical',2,1,'Which topic is most clearly metaphysical?','Whether the self persists when memory changes.','How much rainfall fell yesterday.','How to repair a bicycle chain.','Whether the self persists when memory changes.','Questions about identity and existence are central metaphysical topics.'),Q('metaphysical',3,1,'Choose the adjective in “a ___ debate about free will.”','metaphysical','metaphysics','metaphysically','metaphysical','Metaphysical is the adjective before debate; metaphysics is the field noun.'),Q('metaphysical',4,1,'Which phrase is closest in meaning to metaphysical here?','concerning first principles of reality','describing a measured physical force','relating to weather observation','concerning first principles of reality','Metaphysical inquiry addresses fundamental questions that physical science alone may not settle.') ]});
D('metaplasm',{etymology:E('From Greek metaplasmos “remodeling,” from metaplassō “mold anew”; scientific and linguistic uses apply the sense of alteration.','metaplasm'),contexts:[C('metaplasm.s2','语言学','The editor discussed metaplasm when explaining how an added sound altered an old word form.','编辑解释古词词形如何因增添一个语音而改变时，讨论了词形变化现象。','metaplasm names a linguistic alteration; when introduces its explanatory context.','Metaplasm is a technical count noun in linguistics; it can describe a change by adding or dropping sounds.','This is the linguistic sense; older cell biology also used metaplasm for lifeless cytoplasmic inclusions.')],mistakes:[M('In linguistics, metaplasm means that a word’s form never changes.','In linguistics, metaplasm means an alteration of a word’s form.','The linguistic sense specifically names a form change, such as adding or dropping a sound.','meaning')],quiz:[Q('metaplasm',1,2,'In linguistics, metaplasm is …','a change in a word’s form, such as adding a sound.','a device for viewing tiny cells.','a type of geological era.','a change in a word’s form, such as adding a sound.','The linguistic sense is an alteration of word form through sound addition or loss.'),Q('metaplasm',2,1,'In older cell biology, what could metaplasm refer to?','Lifeless inclusions in the cytoplasm, such as starch grains.','The cell’s living nucleus only.','A complete multicellular animal.','Lifeless inclusions in the cytoplasm, such as starch grains.','Older cell-biology usage applied metaplasm to nonliving cytoplasmic inclusions.'),Q('metaplasm',3,2,'Which example best illustrates linguistic metaplasm?','A form changes after an extra sound is added.','A meteor enters Earth’s atmosphere.','A cell divides into two living cells.','A form changes after an extra sound is added.','Adding a sound changes a word’s form, which fits the linguistic definition.'),Q('metaplasm',4,1,'Which sense of metaplasm is now historical in cell biology?','A lifeless cytoplasmic inclusion.','A word-form change in linguistics.','A large metropolitan region.','A lifeless cytoplasmic inclusion.','That cell-biology use is explicitly older; the linguistic sense is a separate technical use.') ]});
D('metazoan',{etymology:E('From meta- + Greek zōion “animal,” coined for animals made of many differentiated cells, in contrast with single-celled forms.','metazoan'),contexts:[C('metazoan.s1','生物','A sponge is a simple metazoan whose body contains many specialized cells.','海绵是一种简单的后生动物，身体含有许多特化细胞。','a metazoan names an animal; whose clause describes its multicellular organization.','Metazoan is countable as a noun; it can also be an adjective before tissues or cells.','The defining feature is a multicellular animal body, not merely microscopic size.')],mistakes:[M('A single-celled bacterium is a metazoan.','A single-celled bacterium is not a metazoan; metazoans are multicellular animals.','Metazoan refers to animals made of many differentiated cells, not single-celled bacteria.','meaning')],quiz:[Q('metazoan',1,1,'What is a metazoan?','A multicellular animal with differentiated cells.','A single-celled bacterium.','A nonliving mineral crystal.','A multicellular animal with differentiated cells.','Metazoans are animals whose bodies contain many differentiated cells.'),Q('metazoan',2,2,'In “metazoan tissues,” metazoan means …','belonging to multicellular animals.','made of metal.','related to the weather.','belonging to multicellular animals.','The adjective sense means belonging to the metazoans.'),Q('metazoan',3,1,'Which organism is a simple metazoan?','A sponge','A virus particle','A grain of sand','A sponge','Sponges are animals made of many cells and therefore are metazoans.'),Q('metazoan',4,1,'What feature distinguishes a metazoan from a single-celled organism?','Its body consists of many differentiated cells.','It has no biological cells.','It is always smaller than a bacterium.','Its body consists of many differentiated cells.','Multicellularity with differentiated cells is the defining feature in the source sense.') ]});
D('mete',{etymology:E('From Old English metan “to measure”; the verb developed the sense “measure out, distribute,” while the related legal noun means a boundary or boundary marker.','mete'),contexts:[C('mete.s1','法律','The court meted out a modest fine after the first violation.','法院在首次违规后处以了适度罚款。','mete out + penalty is the fixed verbal pattern; out is part of the phrasal verb.','Mete is a transitive verb, commonly used in the past participle meted with out.','The verb means distribute or administer a penalty; the noun mete is an older boundary term.')],mistakes:[M('The court met out a modest fine.','The court meted out a modest fine.','The verb is mete out; its past form is meted, with -ed before out.','verb-form')],quiz:[Q('mete',1,1,'What does the judge do when she “metes out a penalty”?','She administers or assigns the penalty.','She measures the room in metres.','She sends a text message.','She administers or assigns the penalty.','Mete out means to distribute or administer a penalty or reward.'),Q('mete',2,2,'In an old deed, a mete may be …','a boundary or a stone marking a limit.','a short-lived particle.','a type of cell inclusion.','a boundary or a stone marking a limit.','The older noun mete names a boundary or a boundary stone.'),Q('mete',3,1,'Complete the legal phrase: “The court ___ out justice.”','meted','meshed','metered','meted','The established past-tense form in mete out is meted out.'),Q('mete',4,1,'Which sentence uses mete as a verb?','Officials meted out awards to the winners.','The deed lists the ancient metes and bounds.','The scientist studied a meson.','Officials meted out awards to the winners.','Here meted out means distributed awards; the second sentence uses the boundary noun.') ]});
D('meteor',{etymology:E('From Greek meteōron “thing in the air, atmospheric phenomenon,” from meteōros “raised from the earth”; the astronomical sense is the streak of light.','meteor'),contexts:[C('meteor.s1','天文','A bright meteor flashed across the sky during the annual shower.','年度流星雨期间，一颗明亮的流星划过天空。','meteor is the visible streak; during the shower locates when it appeared.','Meteor is a count noun; the streak of light is produced as a meteoroid enters the atmosphere.','The word names the visible atmospheric flash, not the solid object that may reach the ground.')],mistakes:[M('The rock recovered in the field was a meteor, not a meteorite.','The rock recovered in the field was a meteorite; the streak seen in the sky was a meteor.','Meteor is the visible atmospheric flash; a surviving solid object found on the ground is a meteorite.','distinction')],quiz:[Q('meteor',1,1,'What does a person see when a meteor crosses the sky?','A flash or streak as space material enters the atmosphere.','A rock resting on the ground.','A weather instrument at a station.','A flash or streak as space material enters the atmosphere.','A meteor is the visible streak produced by a meteoroid entering the atmosphere.'),Q('meteor',2,1,'Which statement distinguishes a meteor from a meteorite?','A meteor is the light in the sky; a meteorite is solid material that reaches the ground.','A meteor is a weather scientist; a meteorite is a forecast.','They are two spellings of a measuring device.','A meteor is the light in the sky; a meteorite is solid material that reaches the ground.','Meteor names the visible flash; meteorite names the surviving solid remains.'),Q('meteor',3,1,'What might someone watch during a meteor shower?','Many bright streaks appearing in the night sky.','Rocks rising from the ground into space.','Clouds being measured with a thermometer.','Many bright streaks appearing in the night sky.','A meteor shower produces multiple visible atmospheric flashes.'),Q('meteor',4,1,'Which event creates the visible meteor?','A meteoroid heats and glows as it enters the atmosphere.','A meteorite is polished in a museum.','A telescope measures a planet’s mass.','A meteoroid heats and glows as it enters the atmosphere.','The visible flash occurs when the incoming body interacts with the atmosphere.') ]});
D('meteoric',{etymology:E('From meteor + -ic; the rapid-success sense compares a swift rise to a meteor’s sudden brilliant passage across the sky.','meteoric'),contexts:[C('meteoric.s1','通用','The young conductor’s meteoric rise brought invitations from major orchestras within a year.','这位年轻指挥家迅速崛起，一年内就收到了大型管弦乐团的邀请。','meteoric modifies rise and means very rapid and striking success.','Meteoric is an adjective; a meteoric rise is a common collocation.','The sentence uses the rapid-success sense, not the literal relation to meteors or atmospheric dust.')],mistakes:[M('Her sudden success was a meteorological rise to fame.','Her sudden success was a meteoric rise to fame.','Meteorological relates to weather science; meteoric describes something that becomes successful very quickly.','confusable')],quiz:[Q('meteoric',1,1,'A meteoric rise to fame is …','a very rapid rise to success or recognition.','a slow decline in public interest.','a weather report about a falling star.','a very rapid rise to success or recognition.','The figurative adjective describes success or fame achieved very quickly.'),Q('meteoric',2,2,'Which phrase uses the literal space-related sense?','meteoric dust from a passing body','a meteoric rise in sales','a meteoric career in music','meteoric dust from a passing body','The adjective can also mean relating to meteors or their material.'),Q('meteoric',3,1,'Which career description is most likely to be called meteoric?','An unknown singer becomes internationally famous in months.','A researcher publishes one paper every decade.','A shop keeps the same sales for years.','An unknown singer becomes internationally famous in months.','A meteoric rise is remarkably fast and often brilliant.'),Q('meteoric',4,2,'In “meteoric particles entered the upper atmosphere,” meteoric means …','related to meteors or their material.','famous very quickly.','measured in metres.','related to meteors or their material.','Here the adjective describes particles associated with meteors, not a rapid success.') ]});
D('meteorite',{etymology:E('Formed from meteor + -ite for the solid fragment that survives its passage through the atmosphere and reaches the ground.','meteorite'),contexts:[C('meteorite.s1','天文','Researchers found a small iron meteorite embedded in the desert floor.','研究人员在沙漠地面中发现了一块小型铁陨石。','meteorite names the recovered solid object; embedded in the desert floor gives its location.','Meteorite is a count noun and can be classified by material, as in iron meteorite.','A meteorite is the solid remnant on the ground, not the flash of light in the sky.')],mistakes:[M('The glowing streak was a meteorite as it crossed the night sky.','The glowing streak was a meteor as it crossed the night sky.','Meteorite refers to solid material that reaches the ground; the visible atmospheric streak is a meteor.','distinction')],quiz:[Q('meteorite',1,1,'What is a meteorite?','A solid fragment that survives passage through the atmosphere.','The flash of light alone in the upper sky.','A scientist who forecasts the weather.','A solid fragment that survives passage through the atmosphere.','A meteorite is the solid remnant of a meteoroid that reaches the ground.'),Q('meteorite',2,1,'A museum labels a heavy iron space rock “meteorite.” Why?','It is a solid remnant found after falling through the atmosphere.','It is the streak of light seen before dawn.','It measures the speed of a storm.','It is a solid remnant found after falling through the atmosphere.','Meteorite names the material that survives and can be recovered.'),Q('meteorite',3,1,'Which is a meteorite rather than a meteor?','A stone recovered from the impact site.','A bright streak seen for a second in the sky.','A forecast for clear weather.','A stone recovered from the impact site.','The recovered stone is the meteorite; the short-lived light streak is the meteor.'),Q('meteorite',4,1,'Which adjective naturally describes a meteorite by its material?','iron','metaphorical','metropolitan','iron','Meteorites are commonly described by material, as in an iron meteorite.') ]});
D('meteoritic',{etymology:E('From meteorite + -ic, the adjective for things relating to meteorites or their material.','meteoritic'),contexts:[C('meteoritic.s1','天文','The laboratory compared meteoritic iron with iron mined from Earth.','实验室将陨石铁与地球开采的铁进行了比较。','meteoritic modifies iron and identifies its source in meteorites.','Meteoritic is an adjective; do not confuse it with meteoric, which can describe rapid success.','The context concerns material from meteorites, not a meteoric rise or a space flash.')],mistakes:[M('The station provided meteoritic data about wind speed and temperature.','The station provided meteorological data about wind speed and temperature.','Meteoritic relates to meteorites; meteorological relates to weather observation.','confusable')],quiz:[Q('meteoritic',1,1,'Meteoritic iron is iron …','from or associated with meteorites.','used only to measure rainfall.','that describes sudden career success.','from or associated with meteorites.','Meteoritic means relating to meteorites or material in them.'),Q('meteoritic',2,1,'Which phrase uses meteoritic correctly?','meteoritic material in a recovered sample','a meteoritic rise to political office','a meteoritic forecast for tomorrow','meteoritic material in a recovered sample','The adjective describes material related to meteorites.'),Q('meteoritic',3,1,'Choose the adjective for “iron from a meteorite.”','meteoritic','meteoric','meteorological','meteoritic','Meteoritic specifically relates to meteorites and their material.'),Q('meteoritic',4,1,'What does meteoritic modify in “meteoritic composition”?','composition','quickly','under','composition','Meteoritic is an adjective that can modify a noun such as composition.') ]});
D('meteoroid',{etymology:E('From meteor + -oid “resembling or having the form of”; a meteoroid is a small solid fragment in space before atmospheric entry.','meteoroid'),contexts:[C('meteoroid.s1','天文','The telescope tracked a meteoroid while it was still moving through space.','望远镜追踪了一颗仍在太空中运行的流星体。','meteoroid names the space fragment before any atmospheric flash occurs.','Meteoroid is a count noun; entering an atmosphere may produce a meteor.','The object is still in space, so it is a meteoroid rather than a meteor or meteorite.')],mistakes:[M('Before entering the atmosphere, the space fragment was already a meteorite.','Before entering the atmosphere, the space fragment was a meteoroid.','A meteoroid is the fragment in space; meteorite describes solid remains that reach the ground.','distinction')],quiz:[Q('meteoroid',1,1,'What is a meteoroid before it enters an atmosphere?','A fragment moving through space.','A visible atmospheric streak.','A solid rock already recovered on the ground.','A fragment moving through space.','The meteoroid is the space-borne fragment that may later produce a meteor.'),Q('meteoroid',2,1,'What can a meteoroid become as it enters an atmosphere?','A meteor, if it produces a visible flash.','A meteorologist.','A metalwork fitting.','A meteor, if it produces a visible flash.','Atmospheric entry can produce the visible phenomenon called a meteor.'),Q('meteoroid',3,1,'Choose the correct stage: “A small rock is still traveling through interplanetary space.”','meteoroid','meteor','meteorite','meteoroid','A small fragment still in space is a meteoroid.'),Q('meteoroid',4,1,'Which sequence is accurate for a fragment that survives to the ground?','meteoroid in space → meteor in the atmosphere → meteorite on the ground','meteorite in space → meteoroid on the ground → meteor in the air','meteorologist → meteoroid → meteorology','meteoroid in space → meteor in the atmosphere → meteorite on the ground','These terms distinguish the space fragment, the visible atmospheric streak, and the surviving solid remnant.') ]});
D('meteorological',{etymology:E('From meteorology + -ical, ultimately from Greek meteōron “atmospheric phenomenon”; the adjective relates to the scientific study or observation of weather.','meteorological'),contexts:[C('meteorological.s2','通用','The airport relies on a meteorological station to record wind and temperature.','机场依靠气象站记录风速和温度。','meteorological modifies station and identifies equipment or a site used to observe weather.','Meteorological is an adjective; meteorological station is a standard compound.','This is the weather-observation sense, not a claim that the station studies the discipline itself.')],mistakes:[M('The meteorological announced heavy rain during the broadcast.','The meteorologist announced heavy rain during the broadcast.','Meteorological is an adjective; the person who reports weather is a meteorologist.','word form')],quiz:[Q('meteorological',1,1,'Meteorological data usually concern …','weather or the scientific study of the atmosphere.','the manufacture of metal tools.','the growth of multicellular animals.','weather or the scientific study of the atmosphere.','Meteorological describes information relating to weather science.'),Q('meteorological',2,2,'What is a meteorological station used for?','Observing or reporting weather conditions.','Studying metaphors in novels.','Measuring the length of a room only.','Observing or reporting weather conditions.','A meteorological station is used to observe or report weather.'),Q('meteorological',3,1,'Which noun is naturally modified by meteorological?','data','metalloid','metaphorically','data','Meteorological data are observations about weather and the atmosphere.'),Q('meteorological',4,2,'Which device or site may be called meteorological?','a weather-observation station','a weather presenter','a study of word meanings','a weather-observation station','The adjective can describe equipment or a station used to observe weather.') ]});
D('meteorologist',{etymology:E('From meteorology + -ist, with meteorology itself formed from Greek meteōron “atmospheric phenomenon” + -logia “study.”','meteorologist'),contexts:[C('meteorologist.s2','通用','The television meteorologist explained why the storm would turn north overnight.','电视天气播报员解释了风暴为何会在夜间转向北方。','meteorologist is the person explaining the forecast; television identifies the broadcasting role.','Meteorologist is a count noun for a weather scientist or weather broadcaster.','This uses the broadcast sense; the word can also name a scientist who studies and forecasts weather.')],mistakes:[M('The meteorology explained the storm’s path on television.','The television meteorologist explained the storm’s path.','Meteorology is the field or weather conditions; a meteorologist is the person who reports them.','confusable')],quiz:[Q('meteorologist',1,1,'Who is a meteorologist?','A scientist who studies and forecasts weather.','A person who makes alloys from iron.','A specialist in ancient stone tools.','A scientist who studies and forecasts weather.','A meteorologist studies the atmosphere and predicts weather.'),Q('meteorologist',2,2,'A familiar face gives the evening weather report on television. The person is a …','TV meteorologist.','meteorology.','meteorological.','TV meteorologist.','A broadcast weather reporter may also be called a meteorologist.'),Q('meteorologist',3,1,'Which job might a meteorologist do?','Analyze atmospheric data and prepare a forecast.','Classify metaphors in a poem.','Forge a bronze hinge.','Analyze atmospheric data and prepare a forecast.','Scientific analysis of weather and forecasting are core meteorologist duties.'),Q('meteorologist',4,1,'Choose the person noun: “The ___ predicted snow.”','meteorologist','meteorology','meteorological','meteorologist','The person who predicts weather is a meteorologist; meteorology is the field.') ]});
D('meteorology',{etymology:E('From Greek meteōron “thing in the air” + -logia “study”; modern meteorology is the study of the atmosphere, weather, and climate.','meteorology'),contexts:[C('meteorology.s2','通用','The island’s meteorology changes quickly when moist ocean air meets the hills.','湿润海洋气流遇到丘陵时，这座岛的天气状况会迅速变化。','the meteorology of a place means its overall weather conditions; when introduces the cause of change.','Meteorology is uncountable when naming a field or the weather patterns of a place.','This uses the less common local-conditions sense, not the academic discipline.')],mistakes:[M('She studied meteorologist at university.','She studied meteorology at university.','Meteorology names the field of study; meteorologist names the person who studies or reports weather.','confusable')],quiz:[Q('meteorology',1,1,'What does meteorology study as a science?','The atmosphere, weather, and climate.','The structure of metal alloys.','How words change sounds.','The atmosphere, weather, and climate.','Meteorology is the scientific study of weather, climate, and the atmosphere.'),Q('meteorology',2,2,'“The meteorology of the island” refers to …','the island’s overall weather conditions.','a weather scientist who lives there.','a device that measures electricity.','the island’s overall weather conditions.','Meteorology can also mean the combined weather conditions of a place.'),Q('meteorology',3,1,'Which phrase names an academic subject?','a degree in meteorology','a degree in meteorological','a degree in meteorologist','a degree in meteorology','Meteorology is the noun naming the science; meteorological is an adjective and meteorologist a person.'),Q('meteorology',4,2,'A traveler asks about the meteorology of a coastal town. What do they want to know?','Its typical weather conditions.','How to repair its railway.','Its ancient language metaphors.','Its typical weather conditions.','The local-conditions sense concerns the weather of a place considered together.') ]});
D('meter',{etymology:E('From Greek metron “measure,” through Latin and French; American English meter names both the SI length unit and a measuring device.','meter'),contexts:[C('meter.s2','技术','The technician checked the electricity meter before replacing the old wiring.','技术员更换旧线路前检查了电表。','meter names a device that measures and displays use; before replacing gives the sequence.','Meter is a count noun here; the British spelling of the length unit is metre.','This is the measuring-device sense, not the unit of length or the verb “to measure.”')],mistakes:[M('The electricity meter measure how much power the flat uses.','The electricity meter measures how much power the flat uses.','A singular meter takes the third-person singular verb measures.','verb-form')],quiz:[Q('meter',1,1,'One meter is equal to …','100 centimetres.','10 centimetres.','1,000 kilometres.','100 centimetres.','A meter is the length unit equal to 100 centimetres.'),Q('meter',2,2,'What does an electricity meter do?','Measures and displays the electricity used.','Measures the temperature of a room.','Converts a message into a poem.','Measures and displays the electricity used.','A meter can be a device that records and shows the amount consumed.'),Q('meter',3,3,'In “The utility meters the water,” meters means …','measures the water with a meter.','turns the water into metal.','sends the water a message.','measures the water with a meter.','As a verb, meter means to measure something with a meter.'),Q('meter',4,1,'The room is four ___ wide. Which sense is used?','meters, a unit of length','meters, an electricity device','meters, the act of measuring','meters, a unit of length','The room’s width is expressed with the length unit meter.') ]});
D('methane',{etymology:E('Named by chemical nomenclature from methyl + -ane, methane is the simplest alkane and the main component of natural gas.','methane'),contexts:[C('methane.s2','通用','The report tracks methane released from landfills because it is a powerful greenhouse gas.','报告追踪垃圾填埋场排放的甲烷，因为它是一种强效温室气体。','methane released from names the emitted gas; because introduces why it is tracked.','Methane is an uncountable substance noun; emissions or releases can be counted.','The focus is methane in the atmosphere, rather than its use as a fuel.')],mistakes:[M('Methane emissions cool the planet by trapping less heat than air.','Methane emissions warm the planet because methane traps heat in the atmosphere.','Atmospheric methane is a strong greenhouse gas and contributes to warming.','meaning')],quiz:[Q('methane',1,1,'What is methane at room temperature?','A colorless, odorless gas that burns easily.','A solid metal used in wires.','A sweet-smelling liquid that is safe to drink.','A colorless, odorless gas that burns easily.','Methane is a colorless, odorless flammable gas and a fuel.'),Q('methane',2,2,'Why do climate reports track methane emissions?','Methane in the air is a strong greenhouse gas.','Methane is the main ingredient in rainwater.','Methane removes all heat from the atmosphere.','Methane in the air is a strong greenhouse gas.','Released methane contributes to the greenhouse effect.'),Q('methane',3,1,'Methane is the main component of …','natural gas.','stainless steel.','microphone signals.','natural gas.','Natural gas consists largely of methane.'),Q('methane',4,2,'In “cutting methane helps slow global warming,” methane refers to …','the gas released into the atmosphere.','a method for measuring room length.','a person who forecasts weather.','the gas released into the atmosphere.','The environmental sense is methane emissions in the air.') ]});
D('methanol',{etymology:E('The chemical name combines methyl with -ol, the suffix used for alcohols; methanol is also called wood alcohol.','methanol'),contexts:[C('methanol.s1','化学','The lab technician labeled the methanol bottle clearly because the liquid is toxic if swallowed.','实验室技术员清楚标记了甲醇瓶，因为这种液体吞下后有毒。','methanol names the chemical; because introduces the safety reason for clear labeling.','Methanol is an uncountable chemical-substance noun; the label refers to the contents.','Methanol is toxic and must not be confused with ethanol, the alcohol found in drinks.')],mistakes:[M('Methanol is the alcohol used in ordinary drinks and is safe to swallow.','Methanol is toxic if swallowed; ethanol is the alcohol commonly found in drinks.','Methanol is poisonous to drink; confusing it with beverage ethanol is dangerous and factually wrong.','meaning')],quiz:[Q('methanol',1,1,'What is methanol?','The simplest alcohol, also called wood alcohol.','A nonflammable metal alloy.','A vitamin required in large amounts.','The simplest alcohol, also called wood alcohol.','Methanol is the simplest alcohol and is often called wood alcohol.'),Q('methanol',2,1,'Which warning belongs on a methanol container?','Toxic if swallowed.','Safe to drink in small amounts.','Use as a substitute for drinking water.','Toxic if swallowed.','Methanol is toxic if ingested and must not be treated as a beverage.'),Q('methanol',3,1,'Which statement distinguishes methanol from beverage alcohol?','Methanol is toxic; beverage alcohol is ethanol.','Methanol and ethanol are identical names for the same substance.','Methanol is a type of methane gas only.','Methanol is toxic; beverage alcohol is ethanol.','Methanol is distinct from ethanol and is poisonous if drunk.'),Q('methanol',4,1,'Why is methanol called wood alcohol?','It has historically been produced from wood and is an alcohol chemically.','It is a wooden measuring stick.','It is the alcohol made by meteorites.','It has historically been produced from wood and is an alcohol chemically.','Wood alcohol is a traditional name for methanol.') ]});
D('method',{etymology:E('From Latin methodus, from Greek methodos “inquiry, pursuit,” built from meta “after, in pursuit of” + hodos “way, path.”','method'),contexts:[C('method.s1','通用','This method filters the water without using electricity.','这种方法无需用电就能过滤水。','this method names one particular way of doing something; without introduces a condition.','Method is a count noun for a particular procedure or technique.','The example identifies a specific technique rather than orderly behavior in general.')],mistakes:[M('This method are safer than the older one.','This method is safer than the older one.','The singular noun method takes the singular verb is.','grammar')],quiz:[Q('method',1,1,'A method for testing water is …','a particular way of carrying out the test.','a person who writes or sends a message.','a result achieved by applying the procedure.','a particular way of carrying out the test.','Method can mean a particular way of doing something.'),Q('method',2,2,'“There is method in her work” suggests that she works …','in an orderly, planned way.','without any pattern or plan.','only with measuring devices.','in an orderly, planned way.','Method can refer to orderly and planned behavior.'),Q('method',3,1,'Which noun is a natural collocation?','a safer method','a methodly window','a meteor methoded','a safer method','A safer method is a standard phrase for a procedure with less risk.'),Q('method',4,1,'Which question asks about method?','How did the team carry out the experiment?','Who delivered the letter?','What is the color of the metal?','How did the team carry out the experiment?','A method explains the way an action or process is performed.') ]});
D('methodical',{etymology:E('From French methodique and Greek methodikos, from methodos; the adjective originally meant characterized by method.','methodical'),contexts:[C('methodical.s2','通用','Although quiet, the archivist is methodical about labeling every box before shelving it.','这位档案员虽然安静，却总是有条不紊地给每个箱子贴好标签再上架。','methodical describes the person’s consistent, careful way of working.','Methodical is an adjective after be; methodical about + gerund is a common pattern.','The sentence describes a person’s organized habit, not a single procedure’s sequence.')],mistakes:[M('The methodically archivist labels every box before shelving it.','The methodical archivist labels every box before shelving it.','An adjective must modify archivist; methodically is an adverb.','word form')],quiz:[Q('methodical',1,1,'A methodical experiment is conducted …','carefully and in an organized sequence.','without recording any steps.','only in a city subway.','carefully and in an organized sequence.','Methodical describes work done step by step in a careful, organized way.'),Q('methodical',2,2,'A methodical colleague is likely to …','keep careful, organized work habits.','change plans randomly every hour.','avoid making any records.','keep careful, organized work habits.','The adjective can describe a person who is consistently careful and organized.'),Q('methodical',3,1,'Which phrase is a natural collocation?','a methodical approach','a carefully approach','a methodical of approach','a methodical approach','Methodical is an adjective that naturally modifies approach.'),Q('methodical',4,1,'Which word is the adverb form, not the adjective?','methodically','methodical','method','methodically','Methodically is the adverb; methodical is the adjective and method the noun.') ]});
D('methodically',{etymology:E('From methodical + -ly, the adverb form of methodical, ultimately from Greek methodos “inquiry, method.”','methodically'),contexts:[C('methodically.s1','通用','She methodically checked each lock, then recorded the result in her notebook.','她按部就班地检查每一把锁，然后把结果记在笔记本上。','methodically modifies checked and describes a fixed, careful sequence.','The -ly adverb modifies an action verb; each emphasizes that no lock was skipped.','The example focuses on proceeding in order without skipping steps.')],mistakes:[M('She methodical checked every lock before leaving.','She methodically checked every lock before leaving.','The verb checked needs the adverb methodically, not the adjective methodical.','word form')],quiz:[Q('methodically',1,1,'If she methodically checks the locks, she checks them …','in a planned sequence without skipping steps.','only when a storm begins.','by guessing which doors are locked.','in a planned sequence without skipping steps.','Methodically means according to a fixed method and in an orderly way.'),Q('methodically',2,1,'Which verb can methodically naturally modify?','inspect','blue','underneath','inspect','Methodically is an adverb and can modify an action such as inspect.'),Q('methodically',3,1,'Which sentence uses the adverb correctly?','The crew methodically numbered every sample.','The crew methodical numbered every sample.','The crew methodicalness numbered every sample.','The crew methodically numbered every sample.','The adverb methodically describes how the crew numbered the samples.'),Q('methodically',4,1,'What does methodically add to “sorted the files”?','The files were sorted according to an orderly process.','The files were sorted by a weather scientist.','The files were turned into metal.','The files were sorted according to an orderly process.','Methodically explains that the action followed a method rather than happening randomly.') ]});
D('methodism',{etymology:E('The religious name Methodism comes from Methodist, first applied to John and Charles Wesley’s followers because of their strict, systematic practices.','methodist'),contexts:[C('methodism.s2','通用','The reviewer used methodism critically to describe a team that followed its checklist even when the facts changed.','评论者用 methodism 一词批评一个团队：即使事实变化，他们仍照搬清单。','methodism is used figuratively for excessive reliance on fixed rules; even when marks the needed shift in judgment.','Lowercase methodism in the figurative use contrasts with capitalized Methodism for the Christian tradition.','This is the critical “rule-bound practice” sense; the religious movement is a separate sense.')],mistakes:[M('Methodism were founded by John Wesley.','Methodism was founded by John Wesley.','Methodism is a singular name for the Protestant tradition and takes was.','grammar')],quiz:[Q('methodism',1,1,'What is Methodism in its religious sense?','A Protestant tradition founded in the eighteenth century by John Wesley.','A branch of meteorology.','A technique for measuring metal.','A Protestant tradition founded in the eighteenth century by John Wesley.','Methodism names the Protestant movement associated with John Wesley.'),Q('methodism',2,2,'A critic calls a checklist “pure methodism.” What is being criticized?','Excessive dependence on fixed rules instead of judgment.','A lack of all procedures.','The scientific study of weather.','Excessive dependence on fixed rules instead of judgment.','The figurative sense criticizes rigid rule-following.'),Q('methodism',3,1,'Which word names a person belonging to the religious tradition?','Methodist','Methodism','methodical','Methodist','A Methodist is a member; Methodism is the tradition and methodical means orderly.'),Q('methodism',4,1,'Which event is connected with Methodism’s religious history?','John Wesley’s eighteenth-century Protestant movement.','The invention of the microchip.','The naming of a geological era.','John Wesley’s eighteenth-century Protestant movement.','Methodism refers to the Protestant tradition founded in the eighteenth century by Wesley.') ]});
D('methodology',{etymology:E('From method + -logy, through modern Latin methodologia; the word names the principles and procedures of a method.','methodology'),contexts:[C('methodology.s1','学术','The paper describes its methodology so readers can judge how the interviews were conducted.','论文说明了研究方法，以便读者判断访谈是如何进行的。','methodology names the principles and procedures guiding the research; so introduces purpose.','Methodology is usually uncountable when it means a research framework or approach.','The example refers to the plan and reasoning behind research procedures, not one isolated technique.')],mistakes:[M('The paper’s methodology are explained in the opening section.','The paper’s methodology is explained in the opening section.','Methodology is a singular uncountable noun in this sentence and takes is.','grammar')],quiz:[Q('methodology',1,1,'A research methodology explains …','the principles and procedures guiding how the research is done.','the list of weather conditions in a city.','the metal content of a laboratory tool.','the principles and procedures guiding how the research is done.','Methodology describes the framework and procedures behind research.'),Q('methodology',2,1,'Why include a methodology section in a paper?','So readers can understand how the study was designed and carried out.','So readers can learn the author’s home address.','So readers can identify every metaphor in the title.','So readers can understand how the study was designed and carried out.','The section explains research principles and procedures, enabling evaluation and replication.'),Q('methodology',3,1,'Which phrase is natural in academic writing?','research methodology','methodology rainfall','methodologyly clear','research methodology','Research methodology is a standard phrase for the procedures and principles used in a study.'),Q('methodology',4,1,'How is methodology different from method?','Methodology concerns the principles and procedures behind the approach; a method is a particular way.','Methodology means the researcher; method means the study’s conclusion.','They always mean a city railway.','Methodology concerns the principles and procedures behind the approach; a method is a particular way.','A methodology provides the framework; a method is one specific technique.') ]});
D('methyl',{etymology:E('From German methyl or French méthyle, from méthylène; ultimately from Greek methy “wine” + hylē “wood,” a name connected with wood alcohol.','methyl'),contexts:[C('methyl.s1','化学','The chemist attached a methyl group, CH₃, to the aromatic ring.','化学家把一个甲基（CH₃）连接到了芳香环上。','methyl group is the standard phrase; attached to describes a structural bond.','Methyl is a chemical noun for a univalent CH₃ group, not a whole molecule of methanol.','The example uses the chemical group with one carbon and three hydrogen atoms.')],mistakes:[M('The methyl group contains one carbon and two hydrogen atoms, CH₂.','The methyl group contains one carbon and three hydrogen atoms, CH₃.','Methyl is CH₃; CH₂ is a different chemical group.','meaning')],quiz:[Q('methyl',1,1,'A methyl group has the formula …','CH₃: one carbon and three hydrogen atoms.','CH₂: one carbon and two hydrogen atoms.','C₂H₆: two carbon atoms and six hydrogen atoms.','CH₃: one carbon and three hydrogen atoms.','The methyl radical is a univalent group with one carbon and three hydrogens.'),Q('methyl',2,1,'In a molecular diagram, what does a methyl group represent?','A one-carbon group attached to the rest of the molecule.','A complete atom of iron.','A unit of length equal to 100 centimetres.','A one-carbon group attached to the rest of the molecule.','Methyl is a chemical group, often attached to a larger molecular structure.'),Q('methyl',3,1,'Which phrase is standard in chemistry?','a methyl group','a methyl weather station','a methyl of three metres','a methyl group','Methyl commonly appears in the compound phrase methyl group.'),Q('methyl',4,1,'Which statement distinguishes methyl from methanol?','Methyl is a CH₃ group; methanol is a complete alcohol molecule.','Methyl is a gas and methanol is a metal.','They are two spellings of the same weather term.','Methyl is a CH₃ group; methanol is a complete alcohol molecule.','A methyl group is part of molecules, whereas methanol is a chemical substance in its own right.') ]});
D('meticulous',{etymology:E('From Latin meticulosus “fearful,” from metus “fear”; the modern sense developed as anxiety about missing details, then extreme care.','meticulous'),contexts:[C('meticulous.s2','书面','His meticulous review caught every inconsistency, but the report was delivered two days late.','他一丝不苟的审阅发现了每处不一致，但报告晚交了两天。','meticulous modifies review; the but-clause illustrates care so thorough that work becomes slow.','Meticulous is an adjective, often used with about or before a noun.','The example highlights the source’s slower-work nuance as well as attention to detail.')],mistakes:[M('The inspector was meticuluous about checking every label.','The inspector was meticulous about checking every label.','Meticulous has one u after the c and no second u before the final s.','spelling')],quiz:[Q('meticulous',1,1,'A meticulous proofreader is …','very careful about each small detail.','unwilling to read any sentence.','mainly concerned with the weather.','very careful about each small detail.','Meticulous describes great care over details.'),Q('meticulous',2,2,'Why can meticulous planning delay a project?','The care may become so thorough that the work moves slowly.','The plan contains no details at all.','The planner predicts a meteor shower.','The care may become so thorough that the work moves slowly.','A secondary nuance is carefulness so extreme that it slows the work.'),Q('meticulous',3,1,'Which phrase uses meticulous naturally?','meticulous about punctuation','meticulously punctuation','meticulous of punctuation','meticulous about punctuation','Meticulous about + noun is a natural pattern for careful attention.'),Q('meticulous',4,2,'Which description best captures meticulous in a slow, careful context?','Thorough to the point of taking extra time.','Fast because no details are checked.','Untidy and difficult to clean.','Thorough to the point of taking extra time.','This matches the nuance that excessive care can make work slow.') ]});
D('meticulously',{etymology:E('From meticulous + -ly, the adverb form of meticulous, ultimately from Latin meticulosus “fearful.”','meticulously'),contexts:[C('meticulously.s2','通用','The curator meticulously arranged the labels so each artifact could be identified at a glance.','馆长把标签安排得一丝不苟，让每件文物一眼就能辨认。','meticulously modifies arranged and describes careful placement without omissions.','Meticulously is an adverb modifying a verb; meticulous would modify a noun or follow be.','This focuses on carefully arranging something, one of the adverb’s useful contexts.')],mistakes:[M('The curator meticulous arranged the labels beside each artifact.','The curator meticulously arranged the labels beside each artifact.','The verb arranged requires the adverb meticulously, not the adjective meticulous.','word form')],quiz:[Q('meticulously',1,1,'If a list is checked meticulously, it is checked …','with extreme care for small details.','once without looking at it.','only after it is thrown away.','with extreme care for small details.','Meticulously means with great attention to every small detail.'),Q('meticulously',2,2,'A room meticulously decorated is …','carefully arranged so that little is overlooked.','decorated at random without planning.','designed to measure a room’s length.','carefully arranged so that little is overlooked.','The adverb can describe careful making or arrangement.'),Q('meticulously',3,1,'Choose the adverb: “She ___ recorded each specimen’s location.”','meticulously','meticulous','meticulousness','meticulously','The adverb modifies recorded and describes how carefully the work was done.'),Q('meticulously',4,1,'Which action can be performed meticulously?','checking every entry in a ledger','being the word careful','under a desk','checking every entry in a ledger','Meticulously naturally modifies a detailed action such as checking.') ]});
