D('microcosm',{etymology:E('From Greek mikros “small” + kosmos “world”; the word first described a miniature world and later a community reflecting a larger one.','microcosm'),contexts:[C('microcosm.s1','通用','The neighborhood market is a microcosm of the city, with shoppers from every district.','这个街区市场是整座城市的缩影，顾客来自各个城区。','a microcosm of + larger whole; shoppers from every district show the resemblance.','Microcosm is a count noun and is commonly followed by of.','The example tests the reflective “small-scale version of a larger whole” sense.')],mistakes:[M('The school is a microcosm society with students from many regions.','The school is a microcosm of society, with students from many regions.','The standard pattern is a microcosm of something; of introduces the larger whole represented.','collocation')],quiz:[Q("microcosm",1,1,"A school described as a microcosm of society is …","a small community that reflects features of the larger society.","a small world with its own routines but no resemblance to a larger group.","a large city whose population exceeds its entire surrounding region.","a small community that reflects features of the larger society.","A microcosm represents features of a larger community on a smaller scale."),Q('microcosm',2,2,'A village with its own rules and routines can be called a microcosm because it is …','a small world with its own life.','an exact map of the whole planet.','a microscopic fossil.','a small world with its own life.','Microcosm can mean a small, self-contained world.'),Q('microcosm',3,1,'Which construction is natural?','a microcosm of the nation','a microcosm at the nation of','a microcosmic of nation','a microcosm of the nation','The common collocation is a microcosm of a larger place or group.'),Q('microcosm',4,1,'Why can a school represent a microcosm of society?','It contains a small range of people and interactions found in the wider society.','It is physically smaller than every classroom.','It is an instrument for viewing cells.','It contains a small range of people and interactions found in the wider society.','The figurative sense depends on the small community reflecting a larger one.') ]});
D('microeconomics',{etymology:E('Coined in 1948 from micro- + economics for the branch concerned with single factors and individual decisions.','microeconomics'),contexts:[C('microeconomics.s1','经济','Microeconomics can explain why a price changes when demand rises in one local market.','当某个本地市场的需求上升时，微观经济学可以解释价格为何变化。','microeconomics is the discipline; why introduces a question about price and demand in one market.','Microeconomics is an uncountable field noun and contrasts with macroeconomics.','The focus is individual decisions and a single market, not the national economy as a whole.')],mistakes:[M('Microeconomics studies only the whole national economy and never individual markets.','Microeconomics studies individual decisions and markets; macroeconomics studies the economy as a whole.','The claim reverses the distinction: whole-economy questions belong to macroeconomics.','distinction')],quiz:[Q("microeconomics",1,1,"What does microeconomics analyze?","Supply, demand, prices, and individual decision-makers.","Economy-wide inflation and unemployment across a nation.","The overall growth rate of a national economy.","Supply, demand, prices, and individual decision-makers.","Microeconomics focuses on individual agents and markets rather than the whole economy."),Q("microeconomics",2,1,"Which question is a microeconomics question?","How does a price change in one market when demand rises?","How did national inflation change across the whole economy?","How does economy-wide output affect national unemployment?","How does a price change in one market when demand rises?","Microeconomics focuses on a particular market and its decision-makers; the other questions concern the whole economy."),Q('microeconomics',3,1,'Which field contrasts with microeconomics by studying the whole economy?','macroeconomics','microbiology','meteorology','macroeconomics','Macroeconomics studies economy-wide totals, unlike microeconomics’ individual markets and choices.'),Q('microeconomics',4,1,'A study of one household’s choices among goods belongs most directly to …','microeconomics.','metallurgy.','microscopy.','microeconomics.','Individual decision-makers and their choices are central to microeconomics.') ]});
D('microfilm',{etymology:E('Compound of micro- “small” + film, for film holding greatly reduced images of documents; the verb means to photograph onto that film.','microfilm'),contexts:[C('microfilm.s2','通用','The archive microfilmed the fragile newspapers before storing the originals.','档案馆把易损的报纸拍摄到缩微胶片上，然后保存原件。','microfilm is a transitive verb with newspapers as its object.','As a verb, microfilm means to photograph documents onto microfilm; the past tense is microfilmed.','This is the verb sense, distinct from microfilm as the strip of reduced document images.')],mistakes:[M('The archivist microfilmed the papers onto a microscope.','The archivist microfilmed the papers onto microfilm.','Microfilm is the storage film; a microscope is an optical instrument for viewing small objects.','confusable')],quiz:[Q("microfilm",1,1,"What is microfilm as a noun?","Film carrying greatly reduced photographs of documents.","A transparent sheet carrying full-size images of one document.","A digital database of scanned files.","Film carrying greatly reduced photographs of documents.","Microfilm stores greatly reduced document images on film rather than full-size or digital files."),Q("microfilm",2,1,"If a library microfilms old newspapers, it …","photographs the documents onto microfilm.","scans the pages into digital files only.","copies the pages at full size onto ordinary paper.","photographs the documents onto microfilm.","As a verb, microfilm means to photograph documents onto microfilm."),Q('microfilm',3,1,'Where might a researcher find newspapers from a century ago?','On microfilm in an archive.','Inside a microbe.','In a microclimate.','On microfilm in an archive.','Archives use microfilm to preserve greatly reduced document images.'),Q('microfilm',4,2,'Choose the correct past-tense verb: “The library ___ the records.”','microfilmed','microfilmsed','microfilming','microfilmed','The regular past form of microfilm is microfilmed.') ]});
D('microfossil',{etymology:E('Compound of micro- “small” + fossil; it names preserved remains of tiny organisms, such as pollen or foraminifera.','microfossil'),contexts:[C('microfossil.s1','地质','A single microfossil helped the geologist date the layer of sediment.','一枚微体化石帮助地质学家确定了这层沉积物的年代。','microfossil names a preserved tiny organism; helped + object + verb describes its use in dating.','Microfossil is a count noun; its plural is microfossils.','The fossil is a tiny organism’s remains, not a small stone tool or an intact living microbe.')],mistakes:[M('The geologist called a small stone arrowhead a microfossil.','The geologist called preserved remains of a tiny organism a microfossil.','A microfossil is biological material from a tiny organism, not a human-made stone tool.','meaning')],quiz:[Q('microfossil',1,1,'What is a microfossil?','The preserved remains of a tiny organism.','A tool used to look at cells.','A large dinosaur skeleton.','The preserved remains of a tiny organism.','Microfossils are remains of very small organisms, including pollen grains.'),Q('microfossil',2,1,'How can a microfossil help a geologist?','It can help date the sediment layer where it was preserved.','It predicts the weather tomorrow.','It measures electricity use.','It can help date the sediment layer where it was preserved.','The age or type of microfossils can help date geological layers.'),Q('microfossil',3,1,'Which item could be a microfossil?','A preserved pollen grain.','A metal hinge.','A living adult whale.','A preserved pollen grain.','Pollen grains are tiny biological remains that can be preserved as microfossils.'),Q('microfossil',4,1,'What does micro- indicate in microfossil?','The organism or its remains are very small.','The fossil is made of a microphone.','The fossil is a modern circuit.','The organism or its remains are very small.','Micro- signals small size in the compound.') ]});
D('microhabitat',{etymology:E('Compound of micro- “small” + habitat; it names a very small part of a habitat with its own local conditions.','microhabitat'),contexts:[C('microhabitat.s1','生态','The beetle hides in a damp microhabitat beneath the loose bark.','甲虫藏在松动树皮下潮湿的微栖息地里。','microhabitat names the beetle’s small living place; beneath the bark specifies the location.','Microhabitat is a count noun and can be modified by local conditions such as damp.','The word identifies a tiny part of a larger habitat, not the entire ecosystem.')],mistakes:[M('The whole continent is a microhabitat for the beetle.','A damp space beneath a piece of bark can be a microhabitat for the beetle.','A microhabitat is a tiny part of a habitat, not a whole continent.','meaning')],quiz:[Q("microhabitat",1,1,"A microhabitat is …","a tiny part of a larger habitat, such as a rock crevice.","a large ecosystem containing many different habitats.","the entire geographic range of a species across a region.","a tiny part of a larger habitat, such as a rock crevice.","A microhabitat is a very small living environment within a larger habitat."),Q('microhabitat',2,1,'Where might an insect find a microhabitat?','Under a leaf or in a crevice beneath a rock.','In the entire world’s atmosphere.','Inside a subway timetable.','Under a leaf or in a crevice beneath a rock.','Leaf undersides and rock crevices are examples of microhabitats.'),Q('microhabitat',3,1,'Why might a microhabitat differ from the area around it?','It can have its own moisture, shade, or temperature.','It always contains the whole forest.','It is made from microfilm.','It can have its own moisture, shade, or temperature.','A small habitat patch may have local conditions different from its surroundings.'),Q('microhabitat',4,1,'Which phrase uses microhabitat naturally?','a damp microhabitat beneath a log','a microhabitat the whole continent','a microhabitat of city railway','a damp microhabitat beneath a log','Microhabitat is a localized living place such as the moist space under a log.') ]});
D('micron',{etymology:E('Coined in French from Greek mikron, neuter of mikros “small,” for one millionth of a metre; micrometre later replaced it as the formal unit name.','micron'),contexts:[C('micron.s1','科学','The filter removes particles only a few microns wide.','这个过滤器只能滤除宽度仅有几微米的颗粒。','a few microns wide expresses a small measured width; micron is an old unit name.','Micron is a count noun; one micron equals one micrometre, or one millionth of a metre.','The example uses the older name for a micrometre, not a different unit.')],mistakes:[M('A micron is one millionth of a centimetre.','A micron is one millionth of a metre.','A micron equals a micrometre: 10⁻⁶ metre, not 10⁻⁶ centimetre.','measurement')],quiz:[Q("micron",1,1,"A micron is an older name for …","a micrometre, one millionth of a metre.","a millimetre, one thousandth of a metre.","one millionth of a centimetre.","a micrometre, one millionth of a metre.","Micron is an older name for a micrometre, or 10⁻⁶ metre."),Q("micron",2,1,"A fibre several microns thick is …","only a few millionths of a metre thick.","several millimetres thick.","several nanometres thick.","only a few millionths of a metre thick.","A micron is one millionth of a metre, much smaller than a millimetre."),Q("micron",3,1,"Which symbol is associated with a micron or micrometre?","μm","mm","nm","μm","The symbol μm represents a micrometre, historically also called a micron."),Q('micron',4,1,'Why might a modern technical source use micrometre instead of micron?','Micrometre is the formal unit name; micron is the older name.','Micron means a million metres.','Micron names a weather instrument.','Micrometre is the formal unit name; micron is the older name.','Micron is an older unit name that was replaced by micrometre in formal use.') ]});
D('micronutrient',{etymology:E('Compound of micro- “small” + nutrient; it names a nutrient needed in small quantities for health.','micronutrient'),contexts:[C('micronutrient.s1','营养','Iodine is an essential micronutrient that the body needs only in small amounts.','碘是一种必需微量营养素，人体只需要少量。','micronutrient names a required nutrient; only in small amounts explains the micro- quantity.','Micronutrient is a count noun; examples include vitamins and trace minerals.','The word refers to required quantity, not to a nutrient that is unimportant.')],mistakes:[M('Iron is a micronutrient because the body needs it in huge quantities.','Iron is a micronutrient because the body needs it in small quantities.','A micronutrient is required in small amounts; needing it does not mean it is unimportant.','meaning')],quiz:[Q('micronutrient',1,1,'What is a micronutrient?','A nutrient needed in a small quantity for health.','A nutrient needed in the largest possible quantity.','A nutrient that has no effect on health.','A nutrient needed in a small quantity for health.','Micronutrients such as iron or vitamin C are required in small amounts.'),Q('micronutrient',2,1,'Which is an example of a micronutrient?','iron','water in every case','a whole meal','iron','Iron is a micronutrient needed in relatively small quantities.'),Q('micronutrient',3,1,'What does micro- signal in micronutrient?','The amount required is small.','The nutrient is microscopic and never consumed.','The nutrient comes from a microphone.','The amount required is small.','Micro- refers to the small quantity needed, not lack of importance.'),Q('micronutrient',4,1,'A health report calls vitamin C a micronutrient. It means the vitamin is …','needed in small amounts.','unnecessary for the body.','a type of microorganism.','needed in small amounts.','Micronutrients support health even though they are required only in small quantities.') ]});
D('microorganism',{etymology:E('Compound of micro- “small” + organism; the word names an organism such as a bacterium, archaeon, protozoan, or microscopic fungus.','microorganism'),contexts:[C('microorganism.s1','生物','Microorganisms in the soil recycle nutrients from dead plants.','土壤中的微生物会循环利用枯死植物中的养分。','microorganisms is the plural subject; recycle describes their ecological role.','Microorganism is a count noun; its plural is microorganisms.','The category includes many kinds of tiny organisms, not only bacteria.')],mistakes:[M('Every microorganism is visible to the unaided eye.','Most microorganisms are too small to see without special equipment.','The word refers to organisms such as bacteria and microscopic fungi, which generally require magnification to see.','meaning')],quiz:[Q('microorganism',1,1,'Which can be a microorganism?','A bacterium or microscopic fungus.','A full-sized oak tree.','A metal railway carriage.','A bacterium or microscopic fungus.','Microorganisms include bacteria, archaea, protozoa, and microscopic fungi.'),Q('microorganism',2,1,'What can soil microorganisms do?','Recycle nutrients from dead plants.','Measure the soil in metres.','Send sound through a microphone.','Recycle nutrients from dead plants.','Many soil microorganisms decompose organic material and recycle nutrients.'),Q('microorganism',3,1,'Why are many microorganisms viewed with special equipment?','They are too small to see clearly with the unaided eye.','They are made of solid metal.','They live only on distant planets.','They are too small to see clearly with the unaided eye.','Microorganisms are generally microscopic, requiring magnification for observation.'),Q('microorganism',4,1,'How does microorganism differ from microbe?','Both can refer broadly to tiny living things; microorganism emphasizes an organism.','Microorganism means a weather forecast, while microbe means a city.','They have opposite meanings.','Both can refer broadly to tiny living things; microorganism emphasizes an organism.','The terms overlap substantially; microorganism explicitly names an organism.') ]});
D('microphone',{etymology:E('From Greek mikros “small” + phōnē “sound, voice”; a microphone converts sound into electrical signals.','microphone'),contexts:[C('microphone.s1','设备','Please speak into the microphone so the recording captures your voice clearly.','请对着麦克风说话，这样录音才能清楚收录你的声音。','speak into the microphone names how sound reaches the device.','Microphone is a count noun; the device converts sound into electrical signals.','The microphone captures sound; a loudspeaker performs the reverse conversion into audible sound.')],mistakes:[M('A microphone changes electrical signals into sound for the audience.','A loudspeaker changes electrical signals into sound; a microphone converts sound into electrical signals.','The sentence assigns a loudspeaker’s function to a microphone.','confusable')],quiz:[Q('microphone',1,1,'What does a microphone do?','Changes sound into electrical signals.','Changes electrical signals into visible light only.','Measures the length of a room.','Changes sound into electrical signals.','A microphone converts sound waves into electrical signals for recording or amplification.'),Q('microphone',2,1,'Where should a speaker talk during a recording?','Into the microphone.','Under the microclimate.','Through the microscope.','Into the microphone.','Speaking into a microphone allows the device to capture the voice.'),Q('microphone',3,1,'Which device performs the reverse role of a microphone?','A loudspeaker.','A micron.','A microfossil.','A loudspeaker.','A loudspeaker converts electrical signals back into sound.'),Q('microphone',4,1,'Which phrase is natural?','speak into a microphone','speak across a microphone of','microphone the distance in metres','speak into a microphone','A person speaks into a microphone to make the voice easier to record or amplify.') ]});
D('microprocessor',{etymology:E('Compound of micro- “small” + processor; it names a compact electronic processor that carries out instructions and controls a device.','microprocessor'),contexts:[C('microprocessor.s2','通用','The microprocessor controls the washing machine’s cycles and sensor responses.','微处理器控制洗衣机的运行周期和传感器响应。','microprocessor names the central control part; controls takes the machine’s functions as its object.','Microprocessor is a count noun for a chip that processes instructions.','This emphasizes the machine-control sense; a microprocessor is also the chip inside a computer that executes instructions.')],mistakes:[M('The microprocessor is the metal casing that protects the washing machine’s screen.','The microprocessor is the electronic chip that processes instructions and controls the washing machine.','A microprocessor is an electronic processing component, not the machine’s metal casing.','meaning')],quiz:[Q('microprocessor',1,1,'What is a microprocessor inside a computer?','A small chip that carries out instructions.','A strip of film with tiny document images.','A microorganism that causes illness.','A small chip that carries out instructions.','A microprocessor is a compact chip that processes computer instructions.'),Q('microprocessor',2,2,'In a washing machine, the microprocessor may …','control the machine’s actions and cycles.','store old newspapers on film.','measure the room’s length.','control the machine’s actions and cycles.','The central processor in a machine directs its actions.'),Q('microprocessor',3,1,'Which component is closest in meaning to microprocessor?','a tiny instruction-processing chip','a metalworker’s hammer','a weather station','a tiny instruction-processing chip','A microprocessor is a small electronic chip that processes instructions.'),Q('microprocessor',4,1,'Why is the prefix micro- used in microprocessor?','The processor is implemented in a compact chip.','It is a processor for microbes.','It measures one million metres.','The processor is implemented in a compact chip.','Micro- indicates the compact scale of the electronic component.') ]});
D('microscope',{etymology:E('From French microscope, from Greek mikros “small” + skopein “to look at”; the instrument lets the eye examine objects too small to see unaided.','microscope'),contexts:[C('microscope.s2','比喻','After the leak, the company’s repair records came under the microscope of regulators.','发生泄漏后，监管机构对公司的维修记录进行了仔细审查。','under the microscope is a fixed metaphor for close examination by critics or the public.','Microscope is a count noun; under the microscope does not refer to an actual optical instrument here.','This is the figurative scrutiny sense rather than an instrument used to magnify specimens.')],mistakes:[M('The regulators placed the company’s records under a microphone.','The regulators placed the company’s records under a microscope.','A microscope supports close examination; a microphone captures sound.','confusable')],quiz:[Q('microscope',1,1,'What is a microscope used for?','Seeing objects too small for the unaided eye.','Recording a speaker’s voice.','Measuring electricity use.','Seeing objects too small for the unaided eye.','A microscope magnifies tiny specimens for observation.'),Q('microscope',2,2,'A policy put “under the microscope” is being …','examined very closely by the public or critics.','heated in a microwave oven.','measured in microns only.','examined very closely by the public or critics.','The phrase is figurative for intense scrutiny.'),Q('microscope',3,1,'Which instrument would help a biologist inspect tiny cells?','a microscope','a microphone','a microchip','a microscope','A microscope magnifies objects such as cells; the other items perform different functions.'),Q('microscope',4,2,'Why might a public decision come under the microscope?','People are checking it in close detail.','It is physically too small to see.','It is being stored on film.','People are checking it in close detail.','The figurative phrase means close public or critical examination.') ]});
D('microscopic',{etymology:E('From microscope + -ic, from Greek mikros “small” + skopein “to look”; microscopic describes things tiny enough to require a microscope.','microscopic'),contexts:[C('microscopic.s1','科学','The cut was microscopic, yet it stung when salt water touched it.','这个切口小到几乎看不见，但海水碰到时还是刺痛。','microscopic modifies cut and indicates a size visible only with magnification.','Microscopic is an adjective before a noun or after be.','This uses the tiny-size sense; microscopic can also mean related to microscope viewing.')],mistakes:[M('The microscopic scratch was clearly visible from across the football field.','The tiny scratch was clearly visible from across the football field.','Something microscopic is too small to be seen without magnification, so visibility from far away contradicts the word.','meaning')],quiz:[Q('microscopic',1,1,'A microscopic organism is …','so small that it requires magnification to be seen.','larger than a mountain.','made from a city railway.','so small that it requires magnification to be seen.','Microscopic describes things too small to see without a microscope.'),Q('microscopic',2,1,'Which item could be microscopic?','a tiny scratch visible only through magnification','a large stone bridge','a full-sized train','a tiny scratch visible only through magnification','Microscopic can describe an extremely small mark or object.'),Q('microscopic',3,1,'In “microscopic examination,” microscopic means …','related to viewing with a microscope.','carried out at midday.','connected with a major city.','related to viewing with a microscope.','The adjective can describe observation or techniques involving a microscope.'),Q('microscopic',4,1,'Which adjective contrasts with microscopic in size?','macroscopic','metaphorical','metropolitan','macroscopic','Macroscopic describes objects large enough to be seen without a microscope.') ]});
D('microscopy',{etymology:E('From microscope + -y, ultimately from Greek mikros “small” + skopein “to look”; it names the technique or science of using microscopes.','microscopy'),contexts:[C('microscopy.s1','科学','Light microscopy revealed a cell wall that was invisible at low magnification.','光学显微镜观察显示出在低倍放大下看不见的细胞壁。','light microscopy names the observation technique; revealed states what it showed.','Microscopy is an uncountable noun for the technique or science of microscope examination.','The example describes a method of examining specimens, not the instrument itself.')],mistakes:[M('Microscopy are used to examine cells with a microscope.','Microscopy is used to examine cells with a microscope.','Microscopy is a singular uncountable noun and takes is.','grammar')],quiz:[Q('microscopy',1,1,'What is microscopy?','The technique or science of examining specimens with a microscope.','The study of weather and climate.','A tiny electronic processor.','The technique or science of examining specimens with a microscope.','Microscopy is the practice or science of microscope-based observation.'),Q('microscopy',2,1,'Light microscopy is a way to …','examine specimens using visible light and a microscope.','send a sound signal to a speaker.','measure the size of a city.','examine specimens using visible light and a microscope.','The phrase names a microscopy technique using light.'),Q('microscopy',3,1,'Which phrase is natural?','electron microscopy','microscopy a cell','microscopicly study','electron microscopy','Electron microscopy is a standard compound for one microscopy technique.'),Q('microscopy',4,1,'Which word names the instrument rather than the method?','microscope','microscopy','microscopic','microscope','Microscope is the instrument; microscopy names the technique and microscopic is an adjective.') ]});
D('microwave',{etymology:E('Compound of micro- “small” + wave, referring to electromagnetic waves with shorter wavelengths than ordinary radio waves; the cooking oven is named for them.','microwave'),contexts:[C('microwave.s3','生活','We microwaved the rice for a minute before serving dinner.','晚餐上桌前，我们用微波炉把米饭加热了一分钟。','microwaved is the verb meaning cooked or heated food in a microwave oven.','Microwave can be a noun for an oven or electromagnetic wave, and a verb with microwaved as the past tense.','This is the verb sense; the other senses name the appliance and the wave.')],mistakes:[M('He microwave the soup for two minutes before lunch.','He microwaved the soup for two minutes before lunch.','The regular past tense of the verb microwave is microwaved.','verb-form')],quiz:[Q('microwave',1,1,'What is a microwave as a kitchen appliance?','An oven that heats food with short radio waves.','An instrument for viewing cells.','A unit of length equal to a metre.','An oven that heats food with short radio waves.','A microwave oven heats food using microwave radiation.'),Q('microwave',2,2,'In physics, a microwave is …','an electromagnetic wave shorter than a normal radio wave.','a solid rock from space.','a study of single markets.','an electromagnetic wave shorter than a normal radio wave.','The word also names a band of electromagnetic waves.'),Q('microwave',3,3,'If you microwave leftovers, you …','heat or cook them in a microwave oven.','photograph them onto microfilm.','measure them with a meter.','heat or cook them in a microwave oven.','Microwave is also a verb meaning to heat or cook food in the appliance.'),Q('microwave',4,1,'“Put the soup in the microwave” refers to …','the oven used to heat it.','a meteor streak in the sky.','a microorganism in the soup.','the oven used to heat it.','In everyday cooking, the microwave is the heating appliance.') ]});
D('mid',{etymology:E('From Old English mid “middle, midst,” from Proto-Germanic *midja-; it is used before a period or place to mean its middle.','mid'),contexts:[C('mid.s1','通用','The renovation began in midwinter, when the building was already closed to visitors.','翻修工程在隆冬时开始，那时大楼已经对游客关闭。','midwinter places the event around the middle of winter.','Mid is used as a combining form or adjective before a noun, often in compounds without a space.','The word locates a point in a period; it does not function as a standalone noun in this sentence.')],mistakes:[M('The renovation began at the mid of winter.','The renovation began in midwinter.','Use mid- before a period in a compound such as midwinter; “the mid of” is not the standard phrase.','collocation')],quiz:[Q('mid',1,1,'An event held in midwinter takes place …','around the middle of winter.','at the beginning of summer.','above the ground in flight.','around the middle of winter.','Mid before a time period means in its middle.'),Q('mid',2,1,'Which phrase uses mid to locate a point in time?','midweek meeting','middle-class family','midair collision','midweek meeting','Midweek means the middle part of the week.'),Q('mid',3,1,'In a place name or description, mid- can mean …','in the middle of a place.','after the end of a period.','beneath the surface of a sea.','in the middle of a place.','Mid can refer to the middle of a period or place.'),Q('mid',4,1,'Choose the natural expression for the middle of autumn:','mid-autumn','the mid of autumn','midly autumn','mid-autumn','Mid combines with the time word to indicate its middle.') ]});
D('midair',{etymology:E('Compound of mid “middle” + air, used for the space above the ground, especially in the phrase in midair.','midair'),contexts:[C('midair.s1','通用','The balloon burst in midair, scattering bright paper over the field.','气球在半空中爆开，把彩纸撒向田野。','in midair locates an event in the space above the ground.','Midair is most often used in the fixed prepositional phrase in midair.','The sentence describes an object above the ground, not something happening at midday.')],mistakes:[M('The balloon burst in the mid of air over the field.','The balloon burst in midair over the field.','The usual expression is in midair, without the phrase “the mid of air.”','collocation')],quiz:[Q('midair',1,1,'Something suspended in midair is …','in the space above the ground.','under the soil.','at the center of a city.','in the space above the ground.','Midair refers to the air above the ground.'),Q('midair',2,1,'Which phrase is most common?','in midair','at midair of','on the midairly','in midair','The fixed expression is in midair.'),Q('midair',3,1,'A ball bursts in midair. Where does it burst?','While it is still above the ground.','After it is buried underground.','Inside a subway car.','While it is still above the ground.','The word locates an event in the air before an object reaches the ground.'),Q('midair',4,1,'Which event happens in midair?','A dropped sheet catches a gust before landing.','A book rests on the floor.','A train stops at an underground platform.','A dropped sheet catches a gust before landing.','The sheet is still aloft, so the event takes place in midair.') ]});
D('midday',{etymology:E('Compound of mid + day, from Old English mid and dæg; it names the middle of the day, around twelve o’clock.','midday'),contexts:[C('midday.s1','时间','The crew stopped for lunch at midday, when the sun stood high above the harbor.','船员在正午停下来吃午饭，那时太阳高挂在港口上空。','at midday places the break around the middle of the day.','Midday is a count noun for noon or the middle part of the day, often used without an article.','The context means roughly noon, not the entire afternoon.')],mistakes:[M('The crew stopped for lunch at a midday.','The crew stopped for lunch at midday.','Midday is normally used without an article in the time expression at midday.','grammar')],quiz:[Q('midday',1,1,'When is midday?','Around the middle of the day, about twelve o’clock.','At midnight.','At the middle of winter.','Around the middle of the day, about twelve o’clock.','Midday is approximately noon.'),Q('midday',2,1,'Which sentence uses midday correctly?','The train arrives at midday.','The train arrives in the midday of the week.','The train is a midday.','The train arrives at midday.','At midday is the ordinary time phrase.'),Q('midday',3,1,'A midday meal is eaten …','around noon.','just before dawn.','after midnight.','around noon.','Midday refers to the central point of the day.'),Q('midday',4,1,'Which time contrasts with midday?','midnight','midweek','midair','midnight','Midnight is the middle of the night, contrasting with the middle of the day.') ]});
D('middle',{etymology:E('From Old English middel “middle, central,” from Proto-Germanic *medja-; the body sense refers to the waist and nearby area.','middle'),contexts:[C('middle.s3','身体','The tailor measured around his middle before adjusting the jacket.','裁缝在修改夹克前量了他的腰围。','around his middle refers to the waist and surrounding area.','Middle is a noun here; around the middle is a common body-related phrase.','This is the waist sense, distinct from the central part of something or the adjective meaning equally distant.')],mistakes:[M('He is thick around the middle, meaning he is broad at the center of the road.','He is thick around the middle, meaning he is broad at the waist.','In the body sense, middle means the waist and nearby area, not the center of a road.','meaning')],quiz:[Q('middle',1,1,'The middle of a road is …','the part equally far from its edges.','the road’s final destination.','the road’s name.','the part equally far from its edges.','Middle as a noun can mean the central part between edges or ends.'),Q('middle',2,2,'In “the middle seat,” middle means …','equally distant from the two ends or sides.','located outside the row.','belonging to a large city.','equally distant from the two ends or sides.','As an adjective, middle describes a position between two ends or extremes.'),Q('middle',3,3,'“He is thick around the middle” refers to his …','waist and nearby body area.','place in a queue.','position between two historical eras.','waist and nearby body area.','The middle can mean the waist and the area around it.'),Q('middle',4,1,'Which sentence uses middle as a noun for a central part?','The fountain stands in the middle of the square.','She chose the middle seat.','He tightened his belt around the middle.','The fountain stands in the middle of the square.','The middle of the square is its central area; the other examples use adjective and body senses.') ]});
D('middle ages',{etymology:E('The historical name Middle Ages refers to the period regarded by later European scholars as lying between classical antiquity and modern history.','middle ages'),contexts:[C('middle ages.s1','历史','The stone bridge was built in the Middle Ages, several centuries before the industrial era.','这座石桥建于中世纪，比工业时代早了几个世纪。','in the Middle Ages dates the bridge to a European historical period, roughly 500–1500.','The period name is conventionally capitalized and usually takes the definite article the.','The entry refers specifically to the period in European history, not to middle age as a person’s life stage.')],mistakes:[M('The castle dates from the Middle Ages, the European period from about 500 to 1500, and was built in 1820.','The castle dates from the Middle Ages, the European period from about 500 to 1500, and was built in 1320.','A building from 1820 is far later than the Middle Ages; 1320 falls within the approximate period.','meaning')],quiz:[Q('middle ages',1,1,'What period do the Middle Ages name in European history?','Roughly the years from about 500 to 1500.','The years from 1900 to the present.','The Stone Age before recorded history.','Roughly the years from about 500 to 1500.','The source dates the European historical period to approximately 500–1500.'),Q('middle ages',2,1,'A castle built in 1200 was built …','during the Middle Ages.','during the modern industrial era.','before any human settlement existed.','during the Middle Ages.','The year 1200 falls within the approximate medieval period.'),Q('middle ages',3,1,'Which region is specified by the source sense?','Europe','the whole planet in every historical classification','only ancient Greece','Europe','The entry defines the term as a period in European history.'),Q('middle ages',4,1,'Which phrase names this historical period?','the Middle Ages','middle age of a person','midair','the Middle Ages','The Middle Ages is the standard name of the European historical period.') ]});
D('middle-class',{etymology:E('Compound of middle + class; from the eighteenth century it described a social group positioned between the very rich and the poor.','middle-class'),contexts:[C('middle-class.s1','社会','They grew up in a middle-class family that valued education and stable work.','他们在一个重视教育和稳定工作的中产阶级家庭中长大。','middle-class modifies family and describes a social group that is neither very rich nor poor.','Middle-class is a hyphenated adjective before a noun.','The word describes social position and typical experience, not the midpoint of a physical object.')],mistakes:[M('A middle-class family is, by definition, among the very richest people in the country.','A middle-class family is generally neither very rich nor poor.','Middle-class describes the broad social group between the very rich and the poor.','meaning')],quiz:[Q('middle-class',1,1,'A middle-class family is generally …','neither very rich nor poor.','among the very richest households.','a family living in the middle of a train.','neither very rich nor poor.','The adjective describes a social group between the very rich and poor.'),Q('middle-class',2,1,'Which phrase uses middle-class as an adjective?','a middle-class neighborhood','a neighborhood in the middle of class','a middle classically family','a middle-class neighborhood','Middle-class modifies a noun to describe the social group associated with it.'),Q('middle-class',3,1,'What does middle-class describe here?','A social position and typical way of life.','A physical seat located between two others.','A historical era from 500 to 1500.','A social position and typical way of life.','The adjective concerns socioeconomic group, not spatial position or a historical period.'),Q('middle-class',4,1,'Which idea is associated with middle-class in the source sense?','People who are not at either extreme of wealth.','People who all live in the city center.','People who study microscopic life.','People who are not at either extreme of wealth.','The word describes a group between the very rich and poor.') ]});

